window.TREB_STORIES = [{"id":"ST-01","title":"“The son cometh”: white stones over Jerusalem","top10":true,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"AD 70 (Titus's assault on the outermost wall)","place":"Jerusalem, outermost ('first'/Third) wall on the north-west side of the city","text":"During Titus&#x27;s siege of Jerusalem in AD 70 every Roman legion had stone- and dart-throwing engines, but Josephus singled out those of the Tenth Legion as larger and more powerful than the rest.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|5.269\" data-f=\"P3-F001 P3-F002 P3-F004 P3-F006 P3-F007 P3-F008\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Their stones, he wrote, weighed a talent (about 26 kg) and flew “two furlongs and further” (roughly 400 m).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|5.270;arbiv2023evidence|113–15\" data-f=\"P3-F001 P3-F002 P3-F003 P3-F004 P3-F005 P3-F006 P3-F007 P3-F008 P3-F009 P3-F010\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The stones were white, so the defenders could see them coming.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|5.271\" data-f=\"P3-F001 P3-F002 P3-F004 P3-F006 P3-F007 P3-F008\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Watchmen on the towers cried “The son cometh” whenever an engine was loosed, and those in its path threw themselves flat.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|5.272\" data-f=\"P3-F001 P3-F002 P3-F004 P3-F006 P3-F007 P3-F008\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Romans answered by blackening the stones, and then “destroyed many of them at one blow”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|5.273\" data-f=\"P3-F001 P3-F002 P3-F004 P3-F006 P3-F007 P3-F008\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Excavations at the Russian Compound, just outside the line of this outer wall, found 79 ballista stones in a patch about 15 by 5 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"arbiv2023evidence|109\" data-f=\"P3-F003 P3-F005 P3-F009 P3-F010\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Most weighed less than 11 kg, but one weighed 24 kg, close to a talent.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"arbiv2023evidence|114–15\" data-f=\"P3-F003 P3-F005 P3-F009 P3-F010\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A warning cry, a colour change and a counter-measure make a tiny arms race in four sentences, and the archaeology lets us weigh Josephus&#x27;s boast against the stones actually found. The garbled cry (&#x27;son&#x27; for &#x27;stone&#x27;?) is itself a puzzle that scholars have argued over for three centuries.","quote":{"text":"cried out aloud, in their own country language, 'The son cometh:' so those that were in its way stood off, and threw themselves down upon the ground","key":"josephus1895war","pin":"5.272"},"sources":[{"key":"josephus1895war","grade":"A"},{"key":"josephus1737war","grade":"A"},{"key":"arbiv2023evidence","grade":"A"},{"key":"languagehat2026sonnys","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F001","P3-F002","P3-F003","P3-F004","P3-F005","P3-F006","P3-F007","P3-F008","P3-F009","P3-F010"],"caveats":"Josephus&#x27;s weights and ranges are reported, not measured, and he had reason to magnify Roman power; treat &#x27;a talent&#x27; and &#x27;two furlongs and further&#x27; as Medium/Low confidence. Whiston&#x27;s &#x27;engines&#x27; and &#x27;stones&#x27; render Josephus&#x27;s Greek; the Greek engine terms were not checked here. Talent = 26 kg is Arbiv&#x27;s figure citing Shatzman (1995); ancient talent standards varied. &#x27;Two furlongs&#x27; = c. 400 m is Arbiv&#x27;s reckoning (a stadion is roughly 180–200 m); Arbiv also cites Marsden&#x27;s estimate of 350–450 m for Roman machine ranges. The excavated stones are from the battle at the Third Wall (the outermost wall, which Josephus calls the &#x27;first&#x27;); Arbiv links them to Josephus&#x27;s account but no stone can be tied to the Tenth Legion. Only one of 79 stones approaches a talent, so the archaeology supports heavy engines but not routine talent-weight shots. Text of the cry: the Greek manuscripts read &#x27;the son is coming&#x27;. Whiston&#x27;s own footnote (CCEL edition) says the MSS &#x27;all agree in this reading&#x27;, rejects Hudson&#x27;s emendation to &#x27;the arrow/dart cometh&#x27;, notes that Hebrew ben (son) and eben (stone) are alike but argues the Jews spoke &#x27;Chaldee&#x27; (Aramaic), where &#x27;son&#x27; is bar, and mentions Reland&#x27;s report that some saw a messianic &#x27;mystery&#x27; in it. Some printings of Whiston (CCEL; penelope.uchicago.edu) put &#x27;THE STONE COMETH&#x27; in the main text while Lexundria&#x27;s Whiston has &#x27;The son cometh&#x27; — quote the Lexundria wording and explain. Thackeray&#x27;s Loeb (1928) reportedly renders the cry &#x27;Sonny&#x27;s coming&#x27; and notes Reland&#x27;s suggestion that ha-eben (&#x27;the stone&#x27;) was corrupted to habben (&#x27;the son&#x27;); Buth and Pierce (2014) reportedly argue the watchmen shouted a clipped Hebrew &#x27;stone coming&#x27;. Both were seen only as quoted on the Language Hat blog and are listed to fetch; do not cite them until read.","stop":1,"img":"ballista-stones-2.jpg","imgcap":"Roman ballista stone and sling stones, Israel Museum. Photo Gary Todd, CC0."},{"id":"ST-02","title":"Mangonels, miners and a threat to the Dome of the Rock","top10":true,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"20 September – 2 October 1187","place":"Jerusalem (western wall, then the north-eastern corner)","text":"Saladin&#x27;s army besieged Jerusalem from about 20 September 1187, first attacking the western wall.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"brundage1962expugnatione|;dajanishakeel1988medieval|\" data-f=\"P3-F011 P3-F012 P3-F013 P3-F014 P3-F015 P3-F016 P3-F017 P3-F018 P3-F019 P3-F020 P3-F021\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> After about a week it moved to the north-eastern corner.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"dajanishakeel1988medieval|\" data-f=\"P3-F012 P3-F013 P3-F015 P3-F016 P3-F017 P3-F018 P3-F020 P3-F021\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> A Latin eyewitness saw Saladin order engines and “balistas” put up, with olive branches piled between them and the city, while “the Turks unceasingly hurled rocks forcefully against the ramparts”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"brundage1962expugnatione|\" data-f=\"P3-F011 P3-F014 P3-F019\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> According to Ibn al-Athīr, the mangonels were installed overnight and working by morning; al-Qāḍī al-Fāḍil wrote that their fire brought down the tops of the towers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"dajanishakeel1988medieval|\" data-f=\"P3-F012 P3-F013 P3-F015 P3-F016 P3-F017 P3-F018 P3-F020 P3-F021\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Sappers then opened a breach about 30 m long in two days, at the spot where the First Crusaders had entered in 1099.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"dajanishakeel1988medieval|\" data-f=\"P3-F012 P3-F013 P3-F015 P3-F016 P3-F017 P3-F018 P3-F020 P3-F021\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> No one would keep watch for a night, even for 100 besants.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"brundage1962expugnatione|\" data-f=\"P3-F011 P3-F014 P3-F019\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Balian of Ibelin threatened to destroy the Dome of the Rock and kill thousands of Muslim prisoners; the city surrendered on ransom terms on 2 October.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"dajanishakeel1988medieval|\" data-f=\"P3-F012 P3-F013 P3-F015 P3-F016 P3-F017 P3-F018 P3-F020 P3-F021\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The siege that ended crusader Jerusalem combined stone-throwers to clear the towers with miners to drop the wall, and was settled by a hostage threat against the city&#x27;s holiest Muslim shrine. If confirmed, the breach at the place the crusaders had entered in 1099 gives the paper a ready-made symmetry.","quote":{"text":"The Turks unceasingly hurled rocks forcefully against the ramparts.","key":"brundage1962expugnatione","pin":"Brundage 1962, 159–63 (Fordham web text)"},"sources":[{"key":"brundage1962expugnatione","grade":"A"},{"key":"dajanishakeel1988medieval","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F011","P3-F012","P3-F013","P3-F014","P3-F015","P3-F016","P3-F017","P3-F018","P3-F019","P3-F020","P3-F021"],"caveats":"Dates differ by source: De expugnatione gives 20 September for the start of the siege and 26 September for the move; Dajani-Shakeel gives the Arabic sources&#x27; arrival as 15 Rajab 583/21 September and Ernoul&#x27;s as 18 September, and the move after five days (Arab chroniclers) or one week (Ernoul). &#x27;St Stephen&#x27;s Gate&#x27; in medieval sources is today&#x27;s Damascus Gate (Dajani-Shakeel glosses it Bab al-&#x27;Amud). Engine terms: Brundage&#x27;s translation has &#x27;engines&#x27; and &#x27;balistas&#x27;; Dajani-Shakeel uses &#x27;mangonels&#x27; (presumably for Arabic manjanīq). Do not call these &#x27;trebuchets&#x27;, and no source read says counterweight engines were used in 1187. The 30 m breach, the overnight installation and the tower-tops are reported by chroniclers via Dajani-Shakeel (Medium confidence). Her claim that the 1187 breach was at the 1099 entry point is not attributed to a named chronicle in the web text. The web reproduction of Dajani-Shakeel (Fordham, from Studia Palaestina 1988) lacks her footnotes and page numbers and has OCR slips (e.g. &#x27;A.H. 53&#x27; for 583). The Balian threat is given without naming the chronicle; it is usually traced to Ibn al-Athīr, which should be confirmed in D. S. Richards&#x27;s translation (to fetch). Ransom terms differ: Dajani-Shakeel (Arabic sources) gives 10 dinars per man, 5 per woman, 2 per child; De expugnatione gives 10 besants per male aged ten and over, 5 per female, 1 per boy aged seven and under. &#x27;Imad al-Din reportedly counted 15,000 who could not pay (7,000 men, 8,000 women and children), who were enslaved.","stop":9,"img":"damascus-gate.jpg","imgcap":"The Damascus Gate in Jerusalem’s north wall today. Photo DMY, CC BY 3.0."},{"id":"ST-03","title":"Bad Neighbour, Bad Kinsman and the engine of God","top10":true,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"1191 (siege 1189–1191)","place":"Acre (Akko), Levant","text":"At the siege of Acre during the Third Crusade, the stone-throwers had names.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"geoffrey1848itinerary|3.7\" data-f=\"P3-F022 P3-F023 P3-F024 P3-F025 P3-F026 P3-F027 P3-F028\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Itinerary of King Richard reports that the French king, once recovered from illness, built a petraria &#x27;of superior quality&#x27; called &#x27;Bad Neighbour&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"geoffrey1848itinerary|3.7\" data-f=\"P3-F022 P3-F023 P3-F024 P3-F025 P3-F026 P3-F027 P3-F028\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Turkish defenders answered with one they called &#x27;Bad Kinsman&#x27;, which often smashed it, but the king kept rebuilding it until it brought down part of the main wall and shook the tower called Maledictum.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"geoffrey1848itinerary|3.7\" data-f=\"P3-F022 P3-F023 P3-F024 P3-F025 P3-F026 P3-F027 P3-F028\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Another, built at common expense, was called the &#x27;petraria of God&#x27;; beside it a priest preached and collected money to repair it and to hire men to carry stones, and it reportedly shook down about two poles (roughly 10 m) of the tower&#x27;s wall.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"geoffrey1848itinerary|3.7\" data-f=\"P3-F022 P3-F023 P3-F024 P3-F025 P3-F026 P3-F027 P3-F028\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> One stone from King Richard&#x27;s engines was said to have killed twelve men and was carried to Saladin for inspection.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"geoffrey1848itinerary|3.7\" data-f=\"P3-F022 P3-F023 P3-F024 P3-F025 P3-F026 P3-F027 P3-F028\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Four years after Saladin took Jerusalem, crusaders and defenders were naming, crowd-funding and rebuilding their engines like rival champions, which shows how central artillery had become.","quote":{"text":"he had one of superior quality, to which they gave the name of \"Bad Neighbour.\" The Turks also had one they called \"Bad Kinsman,\" which by its violent casts, often broke \"Bad Neighbour\" in pieces","key":"geoffrey1848itinerary","pin":"Book 3, ch. 7 (Bohn 1848, p. 205)"},"sources":[{"key":"geoffrey1848itinerary","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F022","P3-F023","P3-F024","P3-F025","P3-F026","P3-F027","P3-F028"],"caveats":"The 1848 Bohn translation attributes the Itinerarium Peregrinorum et Gesta Regis Ricardi to Geoffrey de Vinsauf; modern scholars reject that attribution (not checked in detail here), and a modern translation (Nicholson 1997) should be compared before quoting in the paper. The translation uses &#x27;petraria&#x27; (petrary, stone-thrower) and &#x27;mangonels&#x27;; it does not say whether these were traction or counterweight machines, so do not call them counterweight trebuchets. The French form &#x27;Malvoisine&#x27; and Latin names were not verified here; we only verified the English &#x27;Bad Neighbour&#x27;, &#x27;Bad Kinsman&#x27; and &#x27;petraria of God&#x27;. Damage claims (part of the main wall, &#x27;two poles&#x27; length&#x27;) and the twelve-man stone are chronicle reports, probably partisan. The 10 m figure assumes the English pole of 16.5 ft (about 5 m); the Latin unit was not checked. The text was read as OCR from the Wellcome Collection scan (page 205-206), so minor OCR errors were silently corrected (e.g. &#x27;petrarise&#x27; for petrariae).","stop":8,"img":"caerphilly.jpg","imgcap":"Replica siege engines at Caerphilly Castle, Wales (an illustration, not Acre). Photo Colin Smith, CC BY-SA 2.0."},{"id":"ST-04","title":"Did trebuchets really knock down walls?","top10":true,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"1097–1291 (argument published 2016 and 2018)","place":"The Latin East (Levant)","text":"In his 2016 Cardiff doctoral thesis, Michael Fulton concluded that Frankish and Muslim armies both knew the traction trebuchet by the end of the 11th century, and that the counterweight trebuchet appeared by the end of the 12th and grew steadily more powerful through the 13th.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fulton2016artillery|vii\" data-f=\"P3-F029 P3-F030 P3-F031\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Yet, he argued, &#x27;even the largest were not effective breaching engines by the time the Franks were pushed out of the Holy Land&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fulton2016artillery|vii\" data-f=\"P3-F029 P3-F030 P3-F031\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Reviewing Fulton&#x27;s 2018 book for De Re Militari, Peter Purton summarised his case: even the biggest counterweight engines could not breach stone walls, and wherever a fortress was taken, it was by other means, &#x27;most often, mining&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"purton2018review|\" data-f=\"P3-F032 P3-F033 P3-F034\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Purton found it &#x27;questionable&#x27; that carved stones of more than 100 kg had no serious physical effect, and noted that Fulton himself &#x27;wavers&#x27;, allowing that the largest engines demolished battlements and threatened the thicker walls below.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"purton2018review|\" data-f=\"P3-F032 P3-F033 P3-F034\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"It challenges the popular image of trebuchets smashing castle walls and suggests their real jobs were stripping defences, supporting assaults and displaying power, which is exactly the question our physics model can test.","quote":{"text":"even the largest were not effective breaching engines by the time the Franks were pushed out of the Holy Land","key":"fulton2016artillery","pin":"vii (abstract)"},"sources":[{"key":"fulton2016artillery","grade":"A"},{"key":"purton2018review","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F029","P3-F030","P3-F031","P3-F032","P3-F033","P3-F034"],"caveats":"This is an argument, not a settled fact. We read the thesis abstract and the open review, not the 2018 Brill book (fulton2018artillery, to fetch), which is the mature version; Purton&#x27;s page references (4, 404) are to the book. Purton also reports that Fulton rejects the &#x27;hybrid trebuchet&#x27; proposed by other historians (notably Chevedden) and argues that the thickening of walls was exaggerated and not caused by artillery. Purton&#x27;s own view: the largest engines may have been built partly to demonstrate their owners&#x27; power. For Jerusalem 1187 this matters: check whether Saladin&#x27;s breach came from mining (the Phase on Jerusalem should verify). The thesis is a CC BY-ND post-print on Cardiff ORCA; only the front matter was readable through our tools.","stop":8,"img":"trebuchet-stones.jpg","imgcap":"Stone shot recovered from the moat at Pevensey Castle. Photo Michael Garlick, CC BY-SA 2.0."},{"id":"ST-05","title":"A counterweight engine drawn for Saladin","top10":true,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"c. 1180–1187 (before Saladin's death in 1193)","place":"Saladin's court (Egypt and Syria); manuscript now in Oxford","text":"The earliest known picture of a counterweight trebuchet comes from the circle of the man who took Jerusalem. Murḍā ibn ʿAlī al-Ṭarṣūsī wrote a military treatise, Tabṣirat arbāb al-albāb, “on the demand of Sultan Salah el-Din”, around 1187 according to a 1965 bibliography.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaky1965preliminary|107–9\" data-f=\"P3-F035 P3-F036 P3-F040\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Its contents include a chapter on “The Mongonel”, beside others on battering rams and siege towers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaky1965preliminary|107–9\" data-f=\"P3-F035 P3-F036 P3-F040\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The historian Michael Fulton dates it to about 1180, and it certainly predates Saladin&#x27;s death in 1193.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"purton2018review|\" data-f=\"P3-F037 P3-F038 P3-F039\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Its drawing remains “the first known illustration of the counterweight engine”, although Fulton judges that “the immaturity of the design is clear”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"purton2018review|\" data-f=\"P3-F037 P3-F038 P3-F039\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> A manuscript of the treatise is in the Bodleian Library, Oxford (MS. Huntington 264), catalogued as a treatise on weaponry written for Saladin.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaky1965preliminary|107–9;bodleian2026treatise|\" data-f=\"P3-F035 P3-F036 P3-F040\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"It ties the machine at the heart of the paper to the man at the heart of its case study: the counterweight trebuchet first appears on paper at Saladin&#x27;s court, in the years around his conquest of Jerusalem.","quote":{"text":"written by Murda ibn (son) Ali ibn Murda al-Tarsusi, on the demand of Sultan Salah el-Din (circa 1187)","key":"zaky1965preliminary","pin":"107–9 (page to confirm against the PDF)"},"sources":[{"key":"zaky1965preliminary","grade":"A"},{"key":"purton2018review","grade":"B"},{"key":"bodleian2026treatise","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F035","P3-F036","P3-F037","P3-F038","P3-F039","P3-F040"],"caveats":"Dating is uncertain: Zaky (1965) says c. 1187; Fulton (via Purton&#x27;s review) c. 1180; Sean Manning&#x27;s Book and Sword page (not used as evidence) gives c. 1167–1187. Purton calls al-Ṭarṣūsī &#x27;the Egyptian&#x27;. The claim that the drawing is the first known illustration of a counterweight engine is Purton&#x27;s summary of Fulton (2018); Fulton itself and Cahen&#x27;s 1947–48 edition of the text were not read (both to fetch). Zaky&#x27;s chapter title &#x27;The Mongonel&#x27; is his English (with OCR errors in the transliterations; quote corrected for diacritics only where shown). Nothing read says that counterweight engines were used at Jerusalem in 1187; Fulton (per Purton) puts their appearance in the last two decades of the twelfth century and argues for their presence in the Third Crusade (1189–92). The paper must not imply Saladin&#x27;s 1187 &#x27;mangonels&#x27; were counterweight trebuchets. Whether MS. Huntington 264 contains the trebuchet drawing was not checked; the Bodleian metadata (via Biblissima) describes it only as a treatise on weaponry written for Saladin, and Zaky&#x27;s text garbles the location as &#x27;Cambridge Bodleian Library, Oxford, Hunt, 264&#x27;.","stop":3,"img":"tarsusi.jpg","imgcap":"A folio of al-Ṭarsūsī’s treatise for Saladin (Bodleian MS Hunt. 264): a crossbow mounted in a shield. Reproduced by David Nicolle, CC BY 4.0."},{"id":"ST-06","title":"The catapult formula with a cube root","top10":true,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"by c. 270 BC (formula); Philo of Byzantium c. 280–220 BC","place":"Hellenistic Greek world (Alexandria; Byzantium)","text":"By about 270 BC Greek engineers, perhaps working for the Ptolemies in Egypt, had turned catapult design into a formula.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"soedel1979ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F042 P3-F044 P3-F046 P3-F047 P3-F049 P3-F050\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Philo of Byzantium wrote that the hole holding the twisted-cord spring was ‘the fundamental basis and unit of measure’ of every engine, fixed ‘by a standard method which could produce the correct proportions at all sizes’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"paasch2024estimation|2\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F043 P3-F045 P3-F049\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> For arrow-shooters the spring’s diameter was one-ninth of the arrow’s length.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"soedel1979ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F042 P3-F044 P3-F046 P3-F047 P3-F049 P3-F050\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> For stone-throwers the diameter in dactyls (about 19.3 mm) was 1.1 times the cube root of 100 times the stone’s weight in minas (about 437 g).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"soedel1979ancient|;maguire2024ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F042 P3-F044 P3-F046 P3-F047 P3-F048 P3-F049 P3-F050\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In SI units that is about 0.13 m times the cube root of the stone’s mass in kilograms.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"paasch2024estimation|3\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F043 P3-F045 P3-F049\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The cube root is physics: a spring’s stored energy grows with the cube of its diameter.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"soedel1979ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F042 P3-F044 P3-F046 P3-F047 P3-F049 P3-F050\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Doubling the shot meant enlarging every part by the cube root of two, the ‘Delian problem’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"maguire2024ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F041 P3-F042 P3-F044 P3-F048\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Two thousand years before dimensional analysis, Greek engineers wrote down a scaling law: build every part in proportion to one module, and size that module to the cube root of the payload. It is the ancient ancestor of this paper&#x27;s size-free comparison of trebuchets.","quote":{"text":"This had to be obtained not by chance or at random, but by a standard method which could produce the correct proportions at all sizes","key":"paasch2024estimation","pin":"2 (quoting Philo, Belopoeica, in Marsden's 1971 translation)"},"sources":[{"key":"soedel1979ancient","grade":"B"},{"key":"paasch2024estimation","grade":"A"},{"key":"maguire2024ancient","grade":"C"},{"key":"athenaeus1928deipnosophists","grade":"A"},{"key":"polybius1923histories","grade":"A"},{"key":"plutarch1917marcellus","grade":"A"},{"key":"vitruvius1826architecture","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F041","P3-F042","P3-F043","P3-F044","P3-F045","P3-F046","P3-F047","P3-F048","P3-F049","P3-F050","P3-F051","P3-F052","P3-F053"],"caveats":"The formula is given in modern notation by modern authors (Soedel &amp; Foley 1979: &#x27;in modern terms&#x27;); the ancient texts (Philo, Belopoeica; Heron, Belopoeica; Vitruvius 10.11) express it in words or tables. The Philo quotation was seen only as quoted by Paasch &amp; Paasch from Marsden (1971); Marsden is on the to-fetch list for a direct pinpoint. Unit values differ slightly between sources: dactyl 19.268 mm (Maguire), &#x27;about 19.8 millimeters&#x27; (Soedel &amp; Foley; possibly a typo or a different standard); mina 436.6 g (Paasch &amp; Paasch), &#x27;about 437 grams&#x27; (Soedel &amp; Foley). Our own check: with 436.6 g and 19.27 mm, 1.1·∛(100M) in dactyls equals 0.130·∛(m in kg) metres, matching Paasch &amp; Paasch&#x27;s eq. 3. Vitruvius&#x27;s table (10.11.3) is in Roman pounds and its figures are partly corrupt (e.g. a 250-pound stone given &#x27;eleven feet and a half&#x27;). Soedel &amp; Foley&#x27;s &#x27;true and accurate solution for optimal design&#x27; assumes engineers maximised projectile kinetic energy; it is an engineering interpretation, not an ancient statement. The &#x27;largest stone-thrower on record&#x27; (three talents, built by Archimedes for Hiero&#x27;s ship) is a literary report (Athenaeus, c. AD 200, quoting an earlier description). Ten-talent stones at Syracuse were, per Polybius, dropped from swinging beams, not thrown (see facts).","stop":2,"img":"roman-ballista-relief.jpg","imgcap":"A cart-mounted bolt-thrower on Trajan’s Column. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-07","title":"Edward I waits for his Warwolf","top10":true,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"21 April – 20 July 1304","place":"Stirling Castle, Scotland","text":"Edward I of England began the siege of Stirling Castle on 21 April 1304.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|180\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> To supply &#x27;the weights of his siege-engines&#x27; he ordered lead stripped from roofs, sparing on churches only the parts above the altars;<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|180\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Historic Environment Scotland notes cartloads of lead arriving from Brechin, Dunfermline and St Andrews.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"macivor2024war|\" data-f=\"P3-F056 P3-F062\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Besides thirteen engines counted by the chronicler Pierre de Langtoft, the king had a new one &#x27;of novel construction&#x27; called War Wolf, &#x27;le Lup de guerre&#x27; in the royal records and &#x27;Ludgar&#x27; in Langtoft&#x27;s French verse.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|181;anglonorman2025ludgar|\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> When the garrison under Sir William Oliphant tried to surrender, Edward ordered that none of his men enter the castle until War Wolf had struck it.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|181;macivor2024war|\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F056 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061 P3-F062\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Langtoft claimed it &#x27;broke down the entire wall&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|181\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The castle surrendered on 20 July 1304.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"rcahms1963stirlingshire|181\" data-f=\"P3-F054 P3-F055 P3-F057 P3-F058 P3-F059 P3-F060 P3-F061\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A king delaying a surrender so he could test his new weapon is a vivid story, and the order to strip lead &#x27;for the weights&#x27; is documentary evidence that some of Edward&#x27;s engines used counterweights.","quote":{"text":"In the midst of these doings the king causes to be built of timber A terrible engine, and to be called Ludgar; And this at its stroke broke down the entire wall.","key":"rcahms1963stirlingshire","pin":"p. 181, quoting Pierre de Langtoft's chronicle in translation (R.S., ii, 355-9)"},"sources":[{"key":"rcahms1963stirlingshire","grade":"A"},{"key":"macivor2024war","grade":"B"},{"key":"anglonorman2025ludgar","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F054","P3-F055","P3-F056","P3-F057","P3-F058","P3-F059","P3-F060","P3-F061","P3-F062"],"caveats":"RCAHMS notes Langtoft &#x27;was not himself present&#x27;, so &#x27;broke down the entire wall&#x27; is a report, probably exaggerated. HES&#x27;s popular blog adds that War Wolf&#x27;s 140 kg missile &#x27;shattered&#x27; the curtain wall and that a gallery was built for the ladies of the court to watch; these are unreferenced in the blog and are not used. The &#x27;thirteen engines&#x27; figure is Langtoft&#x27;s. RCAHMS cites the Calendar of Documents relating to Scotland, vol. ii, no. 1560 for &#x27;le Lup de guerre&#x27; and the order about War Wolf, and Stevenson&#x27;s Documents illustrative of the History of Scotland ii, 475, 481 for the lead order; we read RCAHMS, not those calendars. The lead order was given &#x27;in the following March&#x27; (i.e. March 1304) to the Prince of Wales. RCAHMS says 25 of the garrison marched out with Oliphant &#x27;but others were left inside&#x27;. Do not repeat the common claim that War Wolf was the largest trebuchet ever built; no source for that was checked. The RCAHMS inventory pages were read via ScotlandsPlaces OCR; the volume&#x27;s full title and publisher should be confirmed.","stop":5,"img":"warwolf-model.jpg","imgcap":"A scale model of a trebuchet in the style of War Wolf, Stirling. Photo Ron L. Toms, CC BY 3.0."},{"id":"ST-08","title":"Breaking a wall at Loch Ness","top10":true,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"October–November 1998 (filmed); broadcast 1 February 2000","place":"Urquhart Castle, Loch Ness, Scotland","text":"In autumn 1998 the science series NOVA gathered timber framers beside Urquhart Castle, on Loch Ness, to build two trebuchets with hand tools.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"schupp1998timber|;nova2000secrets|\" data-f=\"P3-F063 P3-F064 P3-F065 P3-F066 P3-F067 P3-F068 P3-F069 P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> One, designed by the engineer Wayne Neel, had a fixed counterweight; the other, by the French carpenter Renaud Beffeyte, swung its counterweight in a hinged box.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nova2000secrets|\" data-f=\"P3-F063 P3-F065 P3-F066 P3-F067 P3-F068 P3-F069 P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Their target was a mock castle wall 1.5 m (5 ft) thick, about 180 m (200 yd) away.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nova2000secrets|;schupp1998timber|\" data-f=\"P3-F063 P3-F064 P3-F065 P3-F066 P3-F067 P3-F068 P3-F069 P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The fixed machine&#x27;s first stone flew about 155 m (170 yd), short of the wall; after the sling was adjusted, a later throw was clocked by radar at about 56 m/s (126–127 mph).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nova2000secrets|;schupp1998timber|\" data-f=\"P3-F063 P3-F064 P3-F065 P3-F066 P3-F067 P3-F068 P3-F069 P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Both machines finally hit: one stone &#x27;busted it right through to the back&#x27;, said Hew Kennedy, and Beffeyte&#x27;s last shot left the wall bulging and cracked.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nova2000secrets|\" data-f=\"P3-F063 P3-F065 P3-F066 P3-F067 P3-F068 P3-F069 P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It aired on PBS on 1 February 2000.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hadingham2000ready|\" data-f=\"P3-F070\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"It is a rare documented modern test in which full-size trebuchets were aimed at a purpose-built medieval-style wall, and it shows the wall could be broken.","quote":{"text":"It confirms what we came here to prove, didn't it? We - they've had a lovely hit, smack in the middle, and it smashed it. And it's busted it right through to the back.","key":"nova2000secrets","pin":"transcript, final siege sequence (Hew Kennedy)"},"sources":[{"key":"nova2000secrets","grade":"C"},{"key":"schupp1998timber","grade":"C"},{"key":"hadingham2000ready","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F063","P3-F064","P3-F065","P3-F066","P3-F067","P3-F068","P3-F069","P3-F070"],"caveats":"The lead said 1997; the trebuchet film was part of the second Secrets of Lost Empires series, filmed in autumn 1998 and broadcast on 1 February 2000. Numbers are a documentary&#x27;s narration and a participant&#x27;s recollection, not a published test report. Hadingham&#x27;s Smithsonian piece is not independent of NOVA (he was NOVA&#x27;s senior science editor from 1998). NOVA calls the balls sandstone; the participant (Schupp&#x27;s article) says limestone. The radar speed is 127 mph in the NOVA narration (&#x27;the heavier ball&#x27;) and 126 mph for the second throw in the newspaper. Wall dimensions beyond thickness (20 ft tall, 14 ft wide) come only from the newspaper. Renaud&#x27;s &#x27;four tons of sand&#x27; (in a 12-ton capacity box) are presumably US short tons – not stated. The documentary frames the project with Edward I&#x27;s &#x27;Warwolf&#x27; at Stirling (1304) – a lead for the medieval group, not evidence here. Lead&#x27;s revision: NOVA&#x27;s narration alone gives the fixed machine&#x27;s 13,000 lb (5.9 t) counterweight, 250 lb (113 kg) balls and Beffeyte&#x27;s &#x27;four tons of sand&#x27;; Schupp (1998) does not give them, so they were taken out of the retelling until a second source confirms them (useful leads for Phase 6).","stop":5,"img":"urquhart-2.jpg","imgcap":"The replica trebuchet at Urquhart Castle, Loch Ness. Photo August Schwerdfeger, CC BY 4.0."},{"id":"ST-09","title":"Nine engines to one, and two would-be witches","top10":true,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"13–15 July 1099","place":"Jerusalem","text":"The artillery fight for Jerusalem in July 1099 was not one-sided. Seeing how many engines the crusaders had built, the defenders strengthened the weaker parts of the wall and, according to the Gesta, raised their towers by night.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|;krey1921gesta|\" data-f=\"P3-F071 P3-F072 P3-F073 P3-F074 P3-F075 P3-F076 P3-F077 P3-F078\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Once the assault began, wrote the eyewitness Raymond of Aguilers, stones were hurled on every side “from the tormenti and the petrahae”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F071 P3-F073 P3-F074 P3-F075 P3-F076 P3-F077\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The crusaders&#x27; machines also threw firebrands: wood dipped in pitch, wax and sulphur, wrapped in straw and tow and bound with an iron band.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F071 P3-F073 P3-F074 P3-F075 P3-F076 P3-F077\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Next morning, Raymond claimed, the defenders had nine or ten machines for every crusader one, and “our machines were now shaken apart by the blows of many stones”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F071 P3-F073 P3-F074 P3-F075 P3-F076 P3-F077\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> He paused for one incident: two women tried to bewitch one of the hurling machines, but a stone crushed them and three slaves.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F071 P3-F073 P3-F074 P3-F075 P3-F076 P3-F077\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The city fell that day, 15 July.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"krey1921gesta|\" data-f=\"P3-F072 P3-F078\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Raymond&#x27;s account shows both sides running stone-throwing batteries, with the defenders out-gunning the attackers, and adds a bizarre human moment: an attempt to fight a machine with spells, ended by the machine&#x27;s own physics.","quote":{"text":"Two women tried to bewitch one of the hurling machines, but a stone struck and crushed them, as well as three slaves, so that their lives were extinguished and the evil incantations averted.","key":"raymond1921historia","pin":"Krey 1921, 242–265 (Fordham web text, 'The Frankish Victory')"},"sources":[{"key":"raymond1921historia","grade":"A"},{"key":"krey1921gesta","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F071","P3-F072","P3-F073","P3-F074","P3-F075","P3-F076","P3-F077","P3-F078"],"caveats":"All of this is Raymond&#x27;s report as translated by Krey (1921); the 9–10 machines for each crusader engine is almost certainly an exaggeration and is Low confidence. Krey leaves the engine terms in Latin (&#x27;tormenti&#x27;, &#x27;petrahae&#x27;, as printed); these should not be silently rendered &#x27;trebuchet&#x27; (they were most likely traction engines, but that is not stated in the text read). Krey&#x27;s English does not say which side the two women were on, whose machine they tried to bewitch, or whose stone killed them; retellings that make them defenders cursing a crusader engine go beyond this translation. Check the Latin (Recueil des historiens des croisades, Occ. III) or the Hill and Hill translation (1968) before saying more; &#x27;three slaves&#x27; is also Krey&#x27;s rendering. The lead about defenders padding the walls with sacks of chaff/straw or hanging beams against stones was not found in the Raymond, Gesta or Fulcher excerpts read (Fulcher, in the same collection, describes defenders hanging beams on ropes in front of a breach to stop rams, not stones); it may be in another chronicler such as Albert of Aachen and is unverified. Raymond&#x27;s Fordham text has OCR errors (e.g. &#x27;bad&#x27; for &#x27;had&#x27;).","stop":3,"img":"siege-of-jerusalem-1099-2.jpg","imgcap":"The capture of Jerusalem in 1099, a late-medieval illumination. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-10","title":"Sulla cuts down the Academy","top10":true,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"86 BC (conventionally 87–86 BC)","place":"Athens and the Piraeus, Greece","text":"When Athens sided with King Mithridates, the Roman general Sulla besieged both the city and its port, the Piraeus.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1916sulla|12.1;appian1899mithridatic|30\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F080 P3-F081 P3-F082 P3-F083 P3-F084 P3-F085\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Appian says Sulla cut down the grove of the Academy and built his largest siege engines there.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|30\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F080 P3-F083\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Plutarch tells it as a timber crisis: many siege-works collapsed under their own weight or were burnt by the defenders’ fire-bolts, so Sulla ‘laid hands upon the sacred groves’, ravaging the Academy, ‘the most wooded of the city’s suburbs’, and the Lyceum.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1916sulla|12.3\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F081 P3-F082 P3-F084 P3-F085\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Working the engines, Plutarch reports, took ten thousand pairs of mules every day.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1916sulla|12.2\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F081 P3-F082 P3-F084 P3-F085\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> To pay for it all, Sulla stripped treasures from the sanctuaries at Epidaurus, Olympia and Delphi.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1916sulla|12.3–4\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F081 P3-F082 P3-F084 P3-F085\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> He took Athens on the Calends (1st) of March;<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1916sulla|14\" data-f=\"P3-F079 P3-F081 P3-F082 P3-F084 P3-F085\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> the siege of the Piraeus is dated to 86 BC.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"campbell2011ancient|697\" data-f=\"P3-F086\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The groves where Plato and Aristotle had taught were turned into siege engines: a vivid measure of how much timber, animal power and money ancient artillery consumed. It shows that a siege train was a logistics problem long before it was a physics problem.","quote":{"text":"he laid hands upon the sacred groves, and ravaged the Academy, which was the most wooded of the city's suburbs, as well as the Lyceum","key":"plutarch1916sulla","pin":"12.3"},"sources":[{"key":"plutarch1916sulla","grade":"A"},{"key":"appian1899mithridatic","grade":"A"},{"key":"campbell2011ancient","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F079","P3-F080","P3-F081","P3-F082","P3-F083","P3-F084","P3-F085","P3-F086"],"caveats":"Both accounts are ancient literary reports, not measurements. Plutarch (c. AD 100) and Appian (2nd century AD) wrote about 200 years after the siege; Plutarch drew partly on Sulla&#x27;s own Memoirs (he cites them for the date of capture, Sulla 14). The two differ in emphasis: Appian places the felling of the Academy grove at the start, as the site where Sulla built his largest engines; Plutarch presents it as a response to timber running out, and adds the Lyceum. &#x27;Ten thousand pairs of mules&#x27; (20,000 animals) daily is a round, uncorroborated figure; treat as Low–Medium confidence. Plutarch&#x27;s &#x27;fire-bolts&#x27; is Perrin&#x27;s translation; the engine terms in the Greek were not checked. Start year 87 BC is the conventional dating; the only A/B source read that gives a year (Campbell 2011) dates the siege of the Piraeus to 86 BC.","stop":2,"img":"sulla.jpg","imgcap":"The so-called bust of Sulla, Glyptothek, Munich. Photo Bibi Saint-Pol, public domain."},{"id":"ST-11","title":"A head thrown three furlongs","top10":false,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"AD 67 (fell July, after 47 days)","place":"Jotapata (Yodefat), Galilee","text":"In AD 67 Vespasian’s army besieged Jotapata (Yodefat) in Galilee, defended by Josephus, the future historian;<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.240\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> it fell in July after 47 days.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"strauss2022siege|\" data-f=\"P3-F087 P3-F094\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Josephus says Vespasian set 160 engines around the town, some throwing stones ‘of the weight of a talent’ (about 26 kg).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.166–167;soedel1979ancient|\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> During one night assault, he writes, the stones knocked the pinnacles off the wall and the corners off towers, and no body of men could stand against them ‘to the last rank’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.243–244\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> A man standing beside Josephus had his head carried away and his skull flung three furlongs, more than half a kilometre.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.245\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> A pregnant woman was struck so hard that her unborn child was carried half a furlong.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.246\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The noise of the engines alone was ‘very terrible’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"josephus1895war|3.247\" data-f=\"P3-F088 P3-F089 P3-F090 P3-F091 P3-F092 P3-F093\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Excavations at Yodefat since 1992 have confirmed heavy fighting.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"strauss2022siege|\" data-f=\"P3-F087 P3-F094\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"It is one of the most vivid eyewitness descriptions of what Roman stone-throwers did to people, written by the defending commander himself. The same army, under Vespasian&#x27;s son Titus, brought these engines to Jerusalem three years later.","quote":{"text":"his head was carried away by such a stone, and his skull was flung as far as three furlongs","key":"josephus1895war","pin":"3.245"},"sources":[{"key":"josephus1895war","grade":"A"},{"key":"strauss2022siege","grade":"B"},{"key":"soedel1979ancient","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F087","P3-F088","P3-F089","P3-F090","P3-F091","P3-F092","P3-F093","P3-F094"],"caveats":"Josephus commanded the defence and wrote to dramatise it; these are reported, not measured, and the distances are physically doubtful (our judgement, not a sourced claim: a &#x27;furlong&#x27; here renders the Greek stadion, roughly 185 m, so three furlongs is about 550–600 m, near or beyond credible stone-thrower ranges). Whiston&#x27;s &#x27;engines&#x27; covers several Greek machine terms that were not checked against the Greek. The number of engines (160) and &#x27;talent&#x27; stones (3.167) are his figures; a Roman talent-class stone is about 26 kg if the Attic talent is meant (talent mass from Soedel &amp; Foley&#x27;s 78 kg for three talents). Strauss gives the fall as July 67 after 47 days and says archaeology confirmed heavy fighting, but does not discuss artillery finds; Aviam&#x27;s excavation report (to fetch) should be checked for ballista stones. Vespasian was wounded in the foot shortly before this night assault (3.236–239), which is why the Romans pressed it so hard.","stop":1,"img":"ballista-stones.jpg","imgcap":"Ballista stones found at Gamla. Photo CC BY-SA 4.0."},{"id":"ST-12","title":"The sultan who pulled down his own walls","top10":false,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"1219–1220 (with sequels in 1229, 1239 and 1244)","place":"Jerusalem","text":"Saladin&#x27;s victory did not leave Jerusalem a fortress for long. After 1187 the Ayyubids built a wall, towers and a moat, partly under al-Muʿaẓẓam ʿĪsā, son of Saladin&#x27;s brother al-ʿAdil.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wekslerbdolah2011early|446\" data-f=\"P3-F095 P3-F097\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> During the Fifth Crusade, fearing that the crusaders would take the city, al-Muʿaẓẓam had its walls demolished, sparing only the Tower of David.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"karakas2024issue|69–70\" data-f=\"P3-F096 P3-F099 P3-F100 P3-F101 P3-F102\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The demolition was carried out in 1219 and again in 1220; fallen masonry at the foot of the walls, including near Herod&#x27;s Gate, still records it.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wekslerbdolah2011early|447\" data-f=\"P3-F095 P3-F097\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> When Frederick II regained the city by treaty in 1229, the walls were not rebuilt.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannica2026crusades|\" data-f=\"P3-F098 P3-F102\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Fighting did not stop. In December 1239 al-Nāṣir Dāwūd&#x27;s army entered the unwalled city, took a crusader-built stronghold after a 21-day siege, starved out the Tower of David, and had miners demolish what remained.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"karakas2024issue|70–71\" data-f=\"P3-F096 P3-F099 P3-F100 P3-F101 P3-F102\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In 1244 Khwarazmian troops captured and sacked Jerusalem.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannica2026crusades|\" data-f=\"P3-F098 P3-F102\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A ruler deliberately destroyed a city&#x27;s defences because a strong wall would help whoever held it next. It also corrects a tempting story: 1187 was not the last siege of Jerusalem in the trebuchet era.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"wekslerbdolah2011early","grade":"A"},{"key":"karakas2024issue","grade":"A"},{"key":"britannica2026crusades","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F095","P3-F096","P3-F097","P3-F098","P3-F099","P3-F100","P3-F101","P3-F102"],"caveats":"The motive (fear that the crusaders would capture a fortified Jerusalem) is from Karakaş (2024), whose footnote cites Arabic chroniclers (Ibn Wāṣil, al-Maqrīzī, Abū al-Fidāʾ and others, per the PDF text); Weksler-Bdolah gives the dates and archaeology but no motive. Karakaş writes in Turkish; translations here are ours. Page numbers for Karakaş are approximate (PDF text extraction gave inconsistent page breaks around pp. 69–71); check against the PDF. The 1239 account (21-day siege of a crusader-built stronghold taken on 7 December; Tower of David garrison under Richard of Argentan held out until supplies ran out and surrendered on terms on 13 December; an-Nāṣir then brought miners and demolished the fortifications and the Tower) is from Karakaş, citing the Rothelin continuation among others. No source read says siege engines were used in 1239, so do not claim a trebuchet siege that year. Britannica adds that the Crusades of 1239–1241 recovered the city by negotiation before the 1244 sack. Thus 1187 was the last siege of a fully walled medieval Jerusalem in the sources read, but not the last siege or capture of the city. The present walls are Ottoman (sixteenth century), which was not verified from an A/B source here.","stop":9,"img":"jerusalem-walls-19c.jpg","imgcap":"Jerusalem from the north, David Roberts, 1839. Cleveland Museum of Art, CC0."},{"id":"ST-13","title":"The Danish trebuchet that kept its aim","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"1989–1992 (built 1989; results published 1992)","place":"Middelaldercentret (Medieval Centre), Nykøbing Falster, Denmark","text":"In summer 1989 the Danish scholar Peter Vemming Hansen led the building of a full-size trebuchet at Nykøbing Falster.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|;middelaldercentretndprojekt|\" data-f=\"P3-F103 P3-F104 P3-F107 P3-F108 P3-F109 P3-F110 P3-F111 P3-F112\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> His team included a smith, a millwright and a rigger; they made models at 1:10 and 1:5 scale and based the frame on a 13th-century ground plan by Villard de Honnecourt.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|75–76\" data-f=\"P3-F104 P3-F105 P3-F106 P3-F107 P3-F108 P3-F110 P3-F111 P3-F114\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Built for a 4,000 kg counterweight, it had only been tried with 2,000 kg by 1992, when its longest shot threw a 15 kg cast-cement ball 168 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|;saimre2006trebuchet|75–76\" data-f=\"P3-F103 P3-F104 P3-F105 P3-F106 P3-F107 P3-F108 P3-F109 P3-F110 P3-F111 P3-F112 P3-F114\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Hansen found that each extra 500 kg of ballast added about 30 m of range.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|\" data-f=\"P3-F103 P3-F104 P3-F107 P3-F108 P3-F109 P3-F110 P3-F111 P3-F112\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Ten or twelve people worked it, firing about every ten minutes, and its shots strayed no more than 2–3 m to either side.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|\" data-f=\"P3-F103 P3-F104 P3-F107 P3-F108 P3-F109 P3-F110 P3-F111 P3-F112\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Scientific American later reported that a medium-sized machine built by the Falsters Minder museum in Denmark grouped its shots inside a 6 m square at 180 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chevedden1995trebuchet|69\" data-f=\"P3-F113\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"It is among the best-documented scholarly reconstructions: it gives measured range, crew size, rate of fire and accuracy, and shows that a trebuchet could be aimed, not just fired.","quote":{"text":"The shots have a remarkable stability of direction even at long range.","key":"hansen1992experimental","pin":"section on trials (original pagination not shown in online copy)"},"sources":[{"key":"hansen1992experimental","grade":"A"},{"key":"saimre2006trebuchet","grade":"A"},{"key":"chevedden1995trebuchet","grade":"B"},{"key":"middelaldercentretndprojekt","grade":"B"},{"key":"middelaldercentretndblide","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F103","P3-F104","P3-F105","P3-F106","P3-F107","P3-F108","P3-F109","P3-F110","P3-F111","P3-F112","P3-F113","P3-F114"],"caveats":"Hansen&#x27;s article was read in a full-text copy hosted on the Tallinn City Museum website, which lacks the original page numbers (Acta Archaeologica 63: 189–268); pinpoints need the printed original. Chevedden&#x27;s &#x27;180 m within a six-metre square&#x27; (1995) does not match Hansen&#x27;s 168 m maximum (1992); it may reflect later shooting with more ballast or another machine – Chevedden gives no details. The &#x27;30 m per 500 kg&#x27; is read from Hansen&#x27;s diagram and is partly extrapolation. The centre now says its first, &#x27;small&#x27; trebuchet was built in 1989 and that a larger machine served for about 25 years until 2021, replaced in 2023; how Hansen&#x27;s 1989 machine relates to these is not fully clear. Saimre&#x27;s derived figures (long arm 5.85 m, short arm 1.2 m, axle 3.15 m, theoretical maximum 490 m, efficiency 0.34) are measurements from Hansen&#x27;s blueprint, not Hansen&#x27;s own numbers. Lead&#x27;s check: Chevedden et al. credit the 180 m machine to &#x27;the Museum of Falsters Minder in Denmark&#x27;; whether it is Hansen&#x27;s machine is not stated.","stop":5,"img":"middelaldercentret.jpg","imgcap":"A trebuchet at the Medieval Centre, Nykøbing Falster. Photo Hubertus45, CC BY-SA 3.0."},{"id":"ST-14","title":"The prince-president's trebuchet","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"1850","place":"Polygone (artillery range) of Vincennes, near Paris, France","text":"In 1850 Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte, then President of the French Republic, ordered a full-size trebuchet to be rebuilt and tested at the Vincennes artillery range.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fino1972machines|34–35\" data-f=\"P3-F115 P3-F116 P3-F118 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Captain Favé ran the trials, and Louis-Napoléon himself designed the sling and its iron tip.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fino1972machines|34–35;hansen1992experimental|\" data-f=\"P3-F115 P3-F116 P3-F117 P3-F118 P3-F119 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124 P3-F126\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The arm was 10.3 m long and the counterweight weighed 4,500 kg, 1,500 kg of it fixed to the arm and 3,000 kg hanging free.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|;saimre2006trebuchet|74–75\" data-f=\"P3-F116 P3-F117 P3-F118 P3-F119 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124 P3-F125 P3-F126\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Favé reported a 24-pound cannonball thrown 175 m, a 22 cm shell 145 m, and shells of 27 and 32 cm 120 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fino1972machines|34–35;saimre2006trebuchet|74–75\" data-f=\"P3-F115 P3-F116 P3-F118 P3-F119 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124 P3-F125 P3-F126\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> No shot strayed more than about 3 m sideways.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"fino1972machines|34–35;hansen1992experimental|\" data-f=\"P3-F115 P3-F116 P3-F117 P3-F118 P3-F119 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124 P3-F126\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> But the short end of the arm was reportedly only 30 cm long, so the counterweight fell just half a metre, and the frame broke after four shots.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hansen1992experimental|;saimre2006trebuchet|74–75\" data-f=\"P3-F116 P3-F117 P3-F118 P3-F119 P3-F120 P3-F121 P3-F122 P3-F123 P3-F124 P3-F125 P3-F126\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A future emperor&#x27;s experiment showed that a huge counterweight is wasted if the arm geometry is wrong, which is exactly the trade-off our optimisation explores.","quote":{"text":"Le tir était d'une rectitude remarquable et il n'y eut jamais 3 m. de déviation latérale.","key":"fino1972machines","pin":"34–35"},"sources":[{"key":"fino1972machines","grade":"A"},{"key":"hansen1992experimental","grade":"A"},{"key":"saimre2006trebuchet","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F115","P3-F116","P3-F117","P3-F118","P3-F119","P3-F120","P3-F121","P3-F122","P3-F123","P3-F124","P3-F125","P3-F126"],"caveats":"Hansen and Saimre call him &#x27;Emperor Napoleon III&#x27;, but in 1850 he was President (he became Emperor in December 1852); Finó correctly says &#x27;Président&#x27;. All three accounts derive from Favé&#x27;s report (published in L.-N. Bonaparte, Études sur le passé et l&#x27;avenir de l&#x27;artillerie, vol. 2, pp. 38–41 n. 1, per Finó), not seen here (to fetch). The 30 cm short arm (beam 10.3 m) comes from Hansen and Saimre only; Finó does not give it and does not mention the collapse. Shells were filled with sand (Hansen, Saimre) or earth (Finó: &#x27;remplie de terre&#x27;). The &#x27;24 lbs&#x27; are presumably French livres (a 24-pounder ball of roughly 12 kg) – our inference, not stated. Saimre&#x27;s half-metre drop height is his own calculation. Lienhard&#x27;s radio script claims Napoleon&#x27;s machine &#x27;threw rocks backward&#x27;; this is contradicted by the reported ranges and was rejected.","stop":5,"img":"viollet-le-duc-trebuchet-2.png","imgcap":"A counterweight trebuchet after Viollet-le-Duc, nineteenth century. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-15","title":"The trebuchet that missed Tenochtitlan","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"1521","place":"Tlatelolco, Tenochtitlan (Mexico City)","text":"During the siege of Tenochtitlan in 1521, with gunpowder running critically low, Hernán Cortés accepted a proposal to build a trebuchet.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chevedden1995trebuchet|71\" data-f=\"P3-F127 P3-F128 P3-F129\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Cortés told Charles V that carpenters built &#x27;a small machine&#x27; and set it on a stone platform in the market square.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"cortes2004catapult|\" data-f=\"P3-F132\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> According to Paul Chevedden and his co-authors, at the first launch the stone &#x27;went straight up, only to return and smash it&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chevedden1995trebuchet|71\" data-f=\"P3-F127 P3-F128 P3-F129\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Nahua account in the Florentine Codex tells it differently: the stone &#x27;did not land on the people, but fell behind the marketplace at Xomolco&#x27;, the Spaniards argued among themselves, and the arm swung back and forth until it came to rest.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"sahagun1993florentine|12.38\" data-f=\"P3-F130 P3-F131\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Mexica named the machine a &#x27;wooden sling&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"sahagun1993florentine|12.38\" data-f=\"P3-F130 P3-F131\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Cortés admitted that &#x27;we concealed from them the defects of the machine&#x27;, pretending that compassion stopped him from using it.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"cortes2004catapult|\" data-f=\"P3-F132\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Often called the last use of a trebuchet in war, it failed, apparently for want of anyone who knew how to set one up, which shows how much the machine depended on craft knowledge; and we can compare the attackers&#x27; and defenders&#x27; versions.","quote":{"text":"And the catapult kept returning back and forth, going one way and then the other; bit by bit it righted itself.","key":"sahagun1993florentine","pin":"Florentine Codex, Book 12, ch. 38 (Lockhart trans., Early Nahuatl Library)"},"sources":[{"key":"chevedden1995trebuchet","grade":"B"},{"key":"sahagun1993florentine","grade":"A"},{"key":"cortes2004catapult","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F127","P3-F128","P3-F129","P3-F130","P3-F131","P3-F132"],"caveats":"The three accounts differ: Chevedden et al. (probably following Bernal Díaz, not checked) say the stone fell back and smashed the machine; the Florentine Codex says it fell behind the marketplace at Xomolco; Cortés says only that it did not work. Chevedden et al. call this &#x27;The last instance of trebuchet use&#x27;; treat that as &#x27;the last well-known recorded attempt&#x27; since later or obscure uses cannot be ruled out. The Florentine Codex was compiled decades later (under Sahagún) and its Nahuatl text uses words rendered &#x27;catapult&#x27; and &#x27;wooden sling&#x27;; the machine type (traction or counterweight) is not stated. Cortés&#x27;s English text on the AHA site is from his third letter (citing Cartas y relaciones, 319-21) but the translator is not identified and two fetches gave slightly different wording, so only a short, consistently returned phrase is quoted. The Cortés letter is self-serving. Bernal Díaz&#x27;s account (Maudslay translation) is to fetch.","stop":10,"img":"nicaea-counterweight.jpg","imgcap":"A counterweight trebuchet as a thirteenth-century illuminator drew it (an illustration). Public domain."},{"id":"ST-16","title":"A bishop describes the first trebuchets","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"c. 586 (some scholars prefer 597)","place":"Thessalonica (Thessaloniki), Byzantine Empire","text":"When an Avar and Slav army besieged Thessalonica in the late sixth century, the city&#x27;s bishop, John, described its stone-throwing engines in the Miracles of St Demetrius.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Stephen McCotter dates the siege to 586, following Paul Lemerle, although some scholars prefer 597.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> John contrasted the inaccurate rope-pulled machines of the Avars with the accurate torsion artillery of the Byzantine defenders.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In Dr Howard-Johnston&#x27;s translation, he saw four-sided frames narrowing towards the top, where massive iron-sheathed cylinders carried beams &#x27;like the beams of a large house&#x27;, each fitted with a sling.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> McCotter calls this &#x27;clearly a description of a trebuchet&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The one thing missing is the crews hauling on the ropes, which he suggests may have been hidden from the bishop&#x27;s view by protective sheaths of iron or, more commonly, leather.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F133 P3-F134 P3-F135 P3-F136 P3-F137 P3-F138\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The main early source for the trebuchet&#x27;s arrival in the Byzantine world was written by a man on the receiving end, and it names the machine&#x27;s anatomy (frame, axle, beam, sling) almost exactly as a modern engineer would.","quote":{"text":"to them there were attached lengths of wood, like the beams of a large house, and these had slings; and when these (the slings) were raised up, they sent out rocks","key":"mccotter2003byzantines","pin":"Miracles of St Demetrius 14.153 (Lemerle ed.), as quoted by McCotter in Howard-Johnston's translation, nn. 8-9"},"sources":[{"key":"mccotter2003byzantines","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F133","P3-F134","P3-F135","P3-F136","P3-F137","P3-F138"],"caveats":"The date is disputed (586 vs 597); McCotter follows Lemerle&#x27;s 586. The Miracles were written by Archbishop John, and McCotter notes (citing Whitby) that the author may have described machines of his own day, so this is evidence of what was reported, not an engineering record. The quotation is McCotter&#x27;s use of an unpublished-looking translation by Dr Howard-Johnston (McCotter n. 9 points to an alternative translation in DeVries, Technology, 134); the Greek wording, including whether the text uses a term like &#x27;petroboloi&#x27;, was not checked here, so we do not use that term. The rope-pullers&#x27; absence is McCotter&#x27;s inference. McCotter&#x27;s piece is a postgraduate essay (Queen&#x27;s University Belfast, 2003) republished on De Re Militari, so tier A/B rather than a peer-reviewed article. The &#x27;traction trebuchet&#x27; identification is modern interpretation.","stop":3,"img":"wujing-traction-2.jpg","imgcap":"A traction trebuchet from the Wujing Zongyao, 1044. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-17","title":"A siege engine in a hundred carts","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"April–May 1291 (transport late winter 1291)","place":"Ḥiṣn al-Akrād (Krak des Chevaliers) to Acre","text":"In 1291 the Mamluk sultan al-Ashraf marched on crusader-held Acre and summoned siege engines from Syria.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaimeche2005hama|;cartwright2018siege|\" data-f=\"P3-F140 P3-F141 P3-F142 P3-F144\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The prince and historian Abu&#x27;l-Fida, then a young emir in the army of Hamah, later wrote that his troops collected a huge engine called &#x27;the Victorious&#x27; at Ḥiṣn al-Akrād, the castle Europeans called Krak des Chevaliers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaimeche2005hama|;cartwright2018siege|\" data-f=\"P3-F140 P3-F141 P3-F142 P3-F144\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Taken to pieces, it filled 100 carts, and Abu&#x27;l-Fida himself had charge of one.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"yule1903book|2:209\" data-f=\"P3-F139 P3-F142 P3-F143\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Rain and snow struck the column between Ḥiṣn al-Akrād and Damascus, and the journey to Acre took about a month.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"zaimeche2005hama|;cartwright2018siege|\" data-f=\"P3-F140 P3-F141 P3-F142 P3-F144\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Later counts of the engines at the siege vary wildly: al-Maqrizi gave 92, Abulfaraj 300 and one Frankish account 666.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"yule1903book|2:209\" data-f=\"P3-F139 P3-F142 P3-F143\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The siege began in April and the Mamluks broke into the city on 18 May 1291.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"cartwright2018siege|;skyhistory2026acre|\" data-f=\"P3-F140 P3-F141 P3-F144\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A single machine so large it needed a hundred carts and a month to move shows the logistics behind &#x27;artillery&#x27;, and the eyewitness who drove one of the carts later became a famous historian.","quote":{"text":"rains and snowstorm struck us between Hisn al-Akrad and Damascus, causing great hardship","key":"zaimeche2005hama","pin":"quoting Abu'l-Fida, RHC Historiens orientaux 1:163-165 (translator not named)"},"sources":[{"key":"yule1903book","grade":"A"},{"key":"zaimeche2005hama","grade":"C"},{"key":"cartwright2018siege","grade":"C"},{"key":"skyhistory2026acre","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F139","P3-F140","P3-F141","P3-F142","P3-F143","P3-F144"],"caveats":"Abu&#x27;l-Fida is a participant but wrote later; the 100 carts and the month&#x27;s journey are his report. Yule (1903) is old but scholarly and gives the 100 carts, the Hamah contingent and Abu&#x27;l-Fida&#x27;s own cart; the name &#x27;the Victorious&#x27;, Ḥiṣn al-Akrād, the weather and the month rest on two independent tier-C sources (Zaimeche on Muslim Heritage, citing the RHC edition; Cartwright on World History Encyclopedia). Zaimeche&#x27;s version of the passage has errors elsewhere (it dates the fall to 17 June 1291 instead of May), and its translator is not named, so the quote should be checked against Gabrieli&#x27;s Arab Historians of the Crusades or Holt&#x27;s translation before use. The Arabic name usually given, al-Manṣūrī, was not verified from an A/B/C source here (only Wikipedia), so it is not used. Al-Maqrizi wrote in the 15th century; all engine counts are reported, and the spread (92-666) shows how unreliable such numbers are. Yule&#x27;s page reference is to the Toyo Bunko digital copy, page 209 of vol. 2 (1903 edition); the printed page number could not be double-checked. Cartwright&#x27;s article also says many oxen died on the way; not used (single source).","stop":3,"img":"castelnaud.jpg","imgcap":"Reconstructed engines at Castelnaud, Dordogne (an illustration). Photo Jebulon, CC0."},{"id":"ST-18","title":"Mangonel: from love charm to siege engine","top10":false,"category":"Words/oddities","date":"626 to 13th century","place":"Byzantium, the Islamic world and western Europe","text":"Medieval writers rarely named their artillery precisely; Stephen McCotter notes that the most technical term in many siege accounts is simply manganon or the Latin machinis.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F145 P3-F148 P3-F149 P3-F151\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Greek mánganon first meant a love charm or trick, and only later a military engine or other device.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"merriamwebster2026mangonel|;etymonline2026trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F146 P3-F147\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Through Medieval Latin manganum and Anglo-French mangunel it became English mangonel, first known in the 13th century.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"merriamwebster2026mangonel|\" data-f=\"P3-F146 P3-F147\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Through Byzantine Greek manganikon, first used in the Chronicon Paschale for the Avar engines at Constantinople in 626, it also became the Arabic mandjanīq (manjanīq).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F145 P3-F148 P3-F149 P3-F151\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> By 1155 an Anglo-Norman text already paired mangonels with perrieres, &#x27;petraries&#x27; or stone-throwers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025mangonel|;anglonorman2025perriere|\" data-f=\"P3-F150\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Historians usually read mangonel as trebuchet, but McCotter, following Bradbury, warns against deciding a machine&#x27;s type from its name.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"mccotter2003byzantines|\" data-f=\"P3-F145 P3-F148 P3-F149 P3-F151\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"One Greek word for a magic charm became the standard name for stone-throwing engines in Greek, Arabic, Latin, French and English, which is why chroniclers&#x27; engine names cannot be trusted to tell us what kind of machine they saw.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"mccotter2003byzantines","grade":"B"},{"key":"merriamwebster2026mangonel","grade":"B"},{"key":"etymonline2026trebuchet","grade":"C"},{"key":"anglonorman2025mangonel","grade":"A"},{"key":"anglonorman2025perriere","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F145","P3-F146","P3-F147","P3-F148","P3-F149","P3-F150","P3-F151"],"caveats":"Merriam-Webster glosses mánganon as a &#x27;charm meant to arouse passion&#x27; and Etymonline as &#x27;any means of tricking or bewitching&#x27;; McCotter instead stresses mechanical senses (lifting devices like cranes using levers and fulcrums). These are compatible (the word covered both) but the order of senses is from MW. McCotter transliterates the Arabic as &#x27;mandjanik&#x27;. McCotter argues that because the Arabic word comes from Greek, the Arabs probably learned the weapon from Byzantium rather than Persia; that is his inference. Modern writers add confusion: the Anglo-Norman Dictionary glosses mangonel as &#x27;catapult for throwing large stones&#x27;, and Wheelis (2002, n. 1) had to explain that de&#x27; Mussi&#x27;s &#x27;catapults&#x27; were &#x27;technically trebuchets&#x27;. Chinese &#x27;pao&#x27; was not verified here and is left out. &#x27;Petrary&#x27; as the English for perriere is the AND&#x27;s gloss.","stop":3,"img":"nicaea-counterweight-2.jpg","imgcap":"A counterweight trebuchet at the siege of Nicaea, 1337 miniature. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-19","title":"Sailors' tools for siege towers: the Genoese at Jerusalem","top10":false,"category":"Jerusalem","date":"June–July 1099","place":"Jaffa (Joppa) and Jerusalem","text":"Besieging Jerusalem in June 1099, the First Crusade lacked water and wood for siege towers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"bennett2013amphibious|134\" data-f=\"P3-F152 P3-F157 P3-F158\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Gesta Francorum noted that wood came “from a considerable distance”.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"krey1921gesta|\" data-f=\"P3-F153\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Raymond of Aguilers saw captive Muslims forced to carry it: fifty or sixty men shouldered a beam that four pairs of oxen could not have dragged.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F154 P3-F155 P3-F156 P3-F159\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Genoese sailors under William Embriaco had lost their ships at Jaffa but saved their ropes, mallets, spikes, axes and hatchets, and these helped Count Raymond build his engines on Mount Zion.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F154 P3-F155 P3-F156 P3-F159\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Matthew Bennett writes that Embriaco had his vessels broken up so that their timber and nails could be rebuilt inland as towers, and that sailors understood ropes, pulleys and cranes.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"bennett2013amphibious|134\" data-f=\"P3-F152 P3-F157 P3-F158\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> On the eve of the final assault, Duke Godfrey and two other leaders had their machines taken apart and carried “almost a mile” to the north side of the city.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"raymond1921historia|\" data-f=\"P3-F154 P3-F155 P3-F156 P3-F159\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Jerusalem&#x27;s capture in 1099 depended on carpentry and rope-work as much as on courage: a ship&#x27;s chandlery became a siege train. It is a reminder that engines were built on site from whatever timber, iron and skilled hands could be found.","quote":{"text":"had been able, nevertheless, to save ropes, mallets, spikes, axes, and hatchets, which were very necessary to us","key":"raymond1921historia","pin":"Krey 1921, 242–265 (Fordham web text, 'The Frankish Victory')"},"sources":[{"key":"raymond1921historia","grade":"A"},{"key":"krey1921gesta","grade":"A"},{"key":"bennett2013amphibious","grade":"B"},{"key":"cartwright2018capture","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F152","P3-F153","P3-F154","P3-F155","P3-F156","P3-F157","P3-F158","P3-F159"],"caveats":"Raymond (an eyewitness with Count Raymond&#x27;s army) says the sailors were surrounded at night by an enemy fleet and abandoned their ships, saving tools and spoils; Bennett says Embriaco had the ships broken up for timber and nails. These are different traditions and Bennett&#x27;s page shows no footnote, so treat &#x27;ships turned into towers&#x27; as Medium confidence and say that the eyewitness speaks of tools, not hulls. Popular accounts (Cartwright 2018, World History Encyclopedia; Wikipedia) say Genoese and English ships arrived on 17 June with rope and timber; the date and the English ships were not found in the eyewitness excerpts read (Raymond mentions English ships earlier in the march, some abandoned or burned). The beam carried by 50–60 men is a reported image, probably rhetorical. &#x27;Almost a mile&#x27; is Krey&#x27;s translation; the conversion to about 1.5 km is approximate. Krey&#x27;s Fordham web text has no page numbers for individual sentences (the collected accounts occupy pp. 242–265 of Krey 1921). The lead about timber hauled from Samaria/Nablus could not be verified in the sources read. Lead&#x27;s check: Raymond says the Duke and the counts of Flanders and Normandy moved their machines, mantlets and platforms overnight to the side of the city &#x27;between the church of St. Stephen and the valley of Josaphat&#x27; (north side), carried in parts &#x27;almost a mile&#x27;; the Gesta says &#x27;to the eastern side&#x27;.","stop":3,"img":"siege-of-jerusalem-1099.jpg","imgcap":"The siege of Jerusalem of 1099 in a fourteenth-century manuscript (BnF Fr. 2824). Public domain."},{"id":"ST-20","title":"Pianos over Shropshire","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"Built c. 1990; reported 1995–2000","place":"Acton Round Hall, near Bridgnorth, Shropshire, England","text":"Around 1990 the landowner Hew Kennedy and his friend Richard Barr built a full-size trebuchet on his estate at Acton Round, near Bridgnorth, Shropshire.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hadingham2000ready|;shropshirestar2008county|\" data-f=\"P3-F160 P3-F161 P3-F162 P3-F165\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It was a 30-tonne machine about 18 m (60 ft) tall.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"shropshirestar2008county|;chevedden1995trebuchet|66–67\" data-f=\"P3-F160 P3-F161 P3-F162 P3-F163 P3-F164 P3-F165\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Scientific American reported that it had thrown a compact car of 476 kg, engine removed, 80 metres using a 30-tonne counterweight, and had lofted loads of up to 500 kg.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chevedden1995trebuchet|66–69\" data-f=\"P3-F160 P3-F162 P3-F163 P3-F164\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> He hurled upright and grand pianos and dead farm animals across his fields.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hadingham2000ready|;shropshirestar2008county|\" data-f=\"P3-F160 P3-F161 P3-F162 P3-F165\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The most famous modern trebuchet was built by an amateur for fun, and gave Scientific American&#x27;s authors full-scale figures for what a big counterweight machine can throw.","quote":{"text":"I did use it for getting rid of dead animals.","key":"shropshirestar2008county","pin":"para. 8 (online article)"},"sources":[{"key":"chevedden1995trebuchet","grade":"B"},{"key":"shropshirestar2008county","grade":"C"},{"key":"hadingham2000ready","grade":"C"},{"key":"lienhardndmodern","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F160","P3-F161","P3-F162","P3-F163","P3-F164","P3-F165"],"caveats":"All performance figures are reported, not measured by the authors who print them. Chevedden et al. give the car as 476 kg thrown 80 m; Hadingham (Smithsonian, 2000) calls it &#x27;a one-ton car&#x27;. &#x27;30 tonnes&#x27; is the machine&#x27;s mass in the Shropshire Star but the counterweight in Scientific American; Lienhard says &#x27;30-ton trebuchet&#x27;. The yard figures and the dead-horse sizing story come from Lienhard&#x27;s radio script, which cites Mapes in the Wall Street Journal (30 July 1991); the WSJ article itself was not seen (to fetch). Build date is inferred from &#x27;a decade ago&#x27; (Hadingham, Jan 2000) and &#x27;about 17 years&#x27; to c. 2006 (Shropshire Star, 2008). Lead&#x27;s revision: single-source details moved out of the retelling: the £10,000 cost and the 17 years before it rotted (Shropshire Star only); the Wall Street Journal ranges (grand piano 125 yd to a 112 lb weight over 235 yd) and the dead-horse sizing story (Lienhard only, citing Mapes 1991, to fetch).","stop":5,"img":"baux-2.jpg","imgcap":"A reconstructed trebuchet at Les Baux-de-Provence (an illustration, not Kennedy’s machine). Photo Federfabbri, CC BY-SA 3.0."},{"id":"ST-21","title":"Spy mail by sling-bullet","top10":false,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"86 BC","place":"The Piraeus, port of Athens","text":"During Sulla’s siege of the Piraeus, Athens’ fortified port, in 86 BC,<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"campbell2011ancient|697\" data-f=\"P3-F168 P3-F169 P3-F170\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> two Athenian slaves inside the walls became Roman informers.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|31\" data-f=\"P3-F166 P3-F167 P3-F168\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> According to Appian, they wrote down everything that happened on lead sling-bullets and slung them into the Roman lines.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|31\" data-f=\"P3-F166 P3-F167 P3-F168\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> One message read: ‘To-morrow the infantry will make a sally in front upon your workers, and the cavalry will attack the Roman army on both flanks’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|31\" data-f=\"P3-F166 P3-F167 P3-F168\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Sulla hid troops in ambush, killed many of the attackers and drove the rest into the sea.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|31\" data-f=\"P3-F166 P3-F167 P3-F168\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Later, Appian says, Sulla’s catapults each discharged ‘twenty of the heaviest leaden balls at one volley’, shaking the defenders’ siege tower until it was pulled back.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"appian1899mithridatic|34\" data-f=\"P3-F166 P3-F167 P3-F168\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The historian Duncan Campbell reads this as a one-off ‘gambit’ of Sulla’s ingenuity, not normal artillery practice.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"campbell2011ancient|698\" data-f=\"P3-F168 P3-F169 P3-F170\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Lead shot carried words before it carried death: the same small projectile served as an airmail letter one week and as catapult grapeshot the next. It is a rare ancient case where we know what a projectile &#x27;said&#x27;.","quote":{"text":"To-morrow the infantry will make a sally in front upon your workers, and the cavalry will attack the Roman army on both flanks.","key":"appian1899mithridatic","pin":"Mith. 31"},"sources":[{"key":"appian1899mithridatic","grade":"A"},{"key":"campbell2011ancient","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F166","P3-F167","P3-F168","P3-F169","P3-F170"],"caveats":"Single ancient source (Appian, 2nd century AD) for both episodes; record as &#x27;reported by Appian&#x27;. Translation issue: White (1899) renders the Greek as &#x27;enclosed their writing in leaden balls&#x27;, but the Greek (πεσσοῖς ἐκ μολύβδου πεποιημένοις ἐγγράφοντες, Mendelssohn&#x27;s text on Perseids) reads more naturally as &#x27;writing/inscribing on pieces made of lead&#x27;; the informers are θεράποντες (&#x27;servants/attendants&#x27;), which White translates &#x27;slaves&#x27;. &#x27;Leaden balls&#x27; are lead sling-bullets (Latin glandes). The &#x27;twenty at one volley&#x27; passage is obscure: Campbell (697–698) notes Marsden thought it meant salvoes rather than twenty balls per shot, and Rihll suggested a one-armed machine or some kind of barrel; Campbell argues lead bullets were not normally catapult ammunition. No archaeological find of a message-bearing bullet from the Piraeus was located.","stop":2,"img":"roman-ballista-relief.jpg","imgcap":"Roman artillery on Trajan’s Column (an illustration). Public domain."},{"id":"ST-22","title":"The fortunate shot that blew up the Parthenon","top10":false,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"26 September 1687 (night of 26–27 September)","place":"Acropolis, Athens","text":"In 1687 a Venetian army under Francesco Morosini besieged the Ottoman garrison on the Acropolis of Athens.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chatziaslanindmorosini|;ehrlich2026athens|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F172 P3-F173 P3-F174 P3-F175 P3-F176 P3-F177 P3-F178 P3-F179\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> On 22 September, a deserter reportedly told the Venetians that the defenders’ ammunition, and their leading men, were inside the Parthenon.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chatziaslanindmorosini|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F172 P3-F173 P3-F174 P3-F175 P3-F176 P3-F178 P3-F179\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> On the night of 26 September a bomb came through an opening in the roof and ignited the gunpowder stored there.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chatziaslanindmorosini|;ehrlich2026athens|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F172 P3-F173 P3-F174 P3-F175 P3-F176 P3-F177 P3-F178 P3-F179\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The blast threw the temple’s marble across the hill, about 300 Turks were reported killed, and the Acropolis burned through the night and the next day.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chatziaslanindmorosini|;acropolismuseumndhistory|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F172 P3-F173 P3-F174 P3-F175 P3-F176 P3-F178 P3-F179 P3-F180\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Morosini called it ‘a fortunate shot’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"ehrlich2026athens|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F174 P3-F177 P3-F178\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Trying to take home the horses of Athena from the west pediment, his men dropped them and smashed them on the rock below.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"ehrlich2026athens|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F174 P3-F177 P3-F178\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The Venetians abandoned Athens the following April.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"chatziaslanindmorosini|\" data-f=\"P3-F171 P3-F172 P3-F173 P3-F174 P3-F175 P3-F176 P3-F178 P3-F179\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A single high-angle gunpowder shell wrecked a temple that had stood for more than two thousand years. It is the gunpowder-age bookend to the Athens story, and a reminder that a mortar is a trebuchet&#x27;s descendant in trajectory.","quote":{"text":"How it dismayed His Excellency to destroy the beautiful temple which had existed three thousand years!","key":"ehrlich2026athens","pin":"letter by a member of General Königsmark's party, quoted in the section on the Acropolis"},"sources":[{"key":"chatziaslanindmorosini","grade":"B"},{"key":"ehrlich2026athens","grade":"B"},{"key":"acropolismuseumndhistory","grade":"B"},{"key":"historytodayndparthenon","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F171","P3-F172","P3-F173","P3-F174","P3-F175","P3-F176","P3-F177","P3-F178","P3-F179","P3-F180"],"caveats":"Details rest on Venetian-side eyewitness letters and reports quoted by modern authors; the original documents were not read (Mommsen 1941 is to fetch). Disputed or uncertain points: who fired the shot (Chatziaslani: &#x27;some claim&#x27; a lieutenant from Lüneburg); where it came from (Britannica says artillery firing from the Hill of the Muses; Chatziaslani says Königsmark placed 15 guns on the Hill of the Muses and five &#x27;enormous mortars&#x27; on the Areopagus); the death toll (about 300 in Chatziaslani and History Today, both reported figures); whether the Venetians deliberately targeted the Parthenon after the deserter&#x27;s report (implied but not stated outright). &#x27;The horses of Athena&#x27; is Britannica&#x27;s phrase for the west-pediment horses. The quote&#x27;s writer is identified only as &#x27;a member of the party&#x27; with Königsmark; other sources name her as a Swedish lady-in-waiting, which was not verified here. &#x27;Temple of Athena&#x27; in Chatziaslani&#x27;s quoted report means the Parthenon.","stop":10,"img":"parthenon-1687-2.jpg","imgcap":"The bombardment of the Acropolis in 1687, engraving after Fanelli, 1695. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-23","title":"Corpses over the walls of Caffa","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"1346 (siege renewed 1345)","place":"Caffa (Feodosiya), Crimea","text":"In 1345 the Mongol khan Janibeg renewed his siege of Caffa, a Genoese trading city in the Crimea, but a year later plague devastated his army and forced him to lift it.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wheelis2002biological|\" data-f=\"P3-F181 P3-F182 P3-F183 P3-F184 P3-F185 P3-F186 P3-F187\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Gabriele de&#x27; Mussi, a Piacenza notary, wrote that the dying besiegers &#x27;ordered corpses to be placed in catapults and lobbed into the city&#x27;, hoping the stench would kill everyone inside.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wheelis2002biological|\" data-f=\"P3-F181 P3-F182 P3-F183 P3-F184 P3-F185 P3-F186 P3-F187\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Mark Wheelis notes in a footnote that the machines were &#x27;technically trebuchets, not catapults&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wheelis2002biological|\" data-f=\"P3-F181 P3-F182 P3-F183 P3-F184 P3-F185 P3-F186 P3-F187\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> He argues that de&#x27; Mussi probably never went to Caffa, yet finds the story plausible; if the bodies were thrown, the attack was probably what carried plague into the city.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wheelis2002biological|\" data-f=\"P3-F181 P3-F182 P3-F183 P3-F184 P3-F185 P3-F186 P3-F187\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> He doubts, however, that refugees from Caffa brought the Black Death to Europe, since infected ships and caravans left the region by several routes; the siege &#x27;probably had no more than anecdotal importance&#x27; in the plague&#x27;s spread.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wheelis2002biological|\" data-f=\"P3-F181 P3-F182 P3-F183 P3-F184 P3-F185 P3-F186 P3-F187\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A trebuchet used as a weapon of biological warfare is one of the most cited trebuchet stories, and Wheelis&#x27;s careful reading separates the plausible part from the myth that it started the Black Death in Europe.","quote":{"text":"But they ordered corpses to be placed in catapults and lobbed into the city in the hope that the intolerable stench would kill everyone inside.","key":"wheelis2002biological","pin":"p. 973 approx.; de' Mussi, Historia de morbo, in Horrox's translation (Horrox 1994, 16-20) as reproduced by Wheelis"},"sources":[{"key":"wheelis2002biological","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F181","P3-F182","P3-F183","P3-F184","P3-F185","P3-F186","P3-F187"],"caveats":"De&#x27; Mussi was probably not present (Wheelis: &#x27;textual evidence suggests that he did not&#x27;), so this is hearsay written in Piacenza. The lead brief said plague &#x27;likely entered other ways&#x27;; that is not Wheelis&#x27;s view: he thinks the corpse-hurling, if it happened, probably was how plague reached the besieged, and his scepticism is about Caffa as the source of Europe&#x27;s Black Death. &#x27;Catapults&#x27; is the translator&#x27;s word; Wheelis&#x27;s footnote says trebuchets and describes trebuchets as counterweight machines, but whether the Golden Horde used traction or counterweight engines is not known from this source. The exact page of the quote within pp. 971-975 was not confirmed (read via Europe PMC full text). Chevedden et al. (1995, 68-69) also tell the story but say plague spread from Kaffa to Europe via Genoese merchants, the view Wheelis questions.","stop":5,"img":"kyeser-bellifortis.jpg","imgcap":"A counterweight trebuchet in Kyeser’s Bellifortis, about 1405 (an illustration). Public domain."},{"id":"ST-24","title":"Six days' work on an Estonian hillfort","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"August 2002","place":"Otepää hillfort, Estonia","text":"In August 2002 Tanel Saimre organised the building of a trebuchet on the hillfort at Otepää, Estonia.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Built from spruce logs with modern tools in six days of three or four hours&#x27; work, it had a 5.5 m beam and a 3.1 m axle height.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It proved more efficient than expected, so the counterweight stayed at 200–300 kg, throwing stones of 2–11.5 kg.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The best shot sent a 5 kg stone 120 m with a 250 kg counterweight, fired from a hilltop about 35 m above the surrounding land.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Allowing for the height, Saimre put the machine&#x27;s efficiency at about 0.5.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Three shots with the same stone landed within 2 m of each other.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|76–77\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Earlier Estonian machines at Lihula (1997) and Varbola (1999) had thrown 8 kg and 10 kg stones 100–120 m with counterweights of 600 and 1,400 kg.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"saimre2006trebuchet|75\" data-f=\"P3-F188 P3-F189 P3-F190 P3-F191 P3-F192 P3-F193 P3-F194 P3-F195 P3-F196 P3-F197\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A small machine built in six days matched the range of much heavier ones, showing that geometry and tuning matter as much as raw counterweight.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"saimre2006trebuchet","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F188","P3-F189","P3-F190","P3-F191","P3-F192","P3-F193","P3-F194","P3-F195","P3-F196","P3-F197"],"caveats":"Measured by the author of the article (experimenter&#x27;s own report). The 120 m includes a 35 m drop below the hilltop; Saimre&#x27;s level-ground equivalent is &#x27;equal or less than&#x27; 85 m. Saimre&#x27;s &#x27;efficiency&#x27; is his own measure (achieved range against theoretical range) and should be defined before reuse. Lihula figures are &#x27;published&#x27; trial shots; Varbola figures come from the builders and are unpublished (&#x27;according to the enthusiasts themselves&#x27;). Page pinpoints come from WebFetch text extraction of the PDF and should be checked against the printed article (pp. 61–80).","stop":5,"img":"traction-replica-2.jpg","imgcap":"A reconstructed trebuchet at Kolomenskoye, Moscow (not Saimre’s machine). CC0."},{"id":"ST-25","title":"Trebuchet: a word for tipping over","top10":false,"category":"Words/oddities","date":"12th-15th centuries","place":"France and England (Anglo-Norman)","text":"The word trebuchet belongs to the Old French and Anglo-Norman verb trebucher, whose meanings include &#x27;to hurl or cast down, plunge, cause to fall&#x27;, &#x27;to strike down&#x27; and &#x27;to overturn, make capsize&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025trebucher|\" data-f=\"P3-F198\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> English borrowed the noun from Anglo-French as Middle English trebochet, and Merriam-Webster dates its first known English use to the 15th century.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"merriamwebster2026trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F199\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In Anglo-Norman the noun had three senses.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F200 P3-F201 P3-F202\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Besides a &#x27;siege engine for throwing stones or heavy projectiles from a sling&#x27;, a trebuchet was a &#x27;bird-snare, trap for catching small birds&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F200 P3-F201 P3-F202\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It could also mean the right to keep a cucking-stool, a device for exposing an offender to public ridicule; in 1275 the Abbot of Westminster claimed such a right.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F200 P3-F201 P3-F202\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> What the three share is a beam or seat that tips over.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"anglonorman2025trebucher|;anglonorman2025trebuchet|\" data-f=\"P3-F198 P3-F200 P3-F201 P3-F202\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The name describes the motion, not the target: the machine was named for tipping and falling, the same action as a bird-trap that drops shut or a seat that dunks an offender.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"anglonorman2025trebuchet","grade":"A"},{"key":"anglonorman2025trebucher","grade":"A"},{"key":"merriamwebster2026trebuchet","grade":"B"},{"key":"etymonline2026trebuchet","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F198","P3-F199","P3-F200","P3-F201","P3-F202"],"caveats":"The last sentence is an interpretive link drawn from the dictionary senses, not a claim made by the Anglo-Norman Dictionary; present it as an observation. The AND dates the siege-engine sense to the 13th century and the bird-snare to the later 13th century; the cucking-stool citation (1275) is in a Latin legal text (&#x27;collistridium, tresbuchet&#x27;). The Online Etymology Dictionary (tier C) adds that the word derives from &#x27;tra-&#x27; + Old French &#x27;buc&#x27; (trunk), that the noun is attested in Old French in the 12th century, and that it was obsolete from the 16th century and revived by antiquarians in the 18th; these points are not corroborated by an A/B source here and are not used in the retelling. Merriam-Webster also lists the variant &#x27;trebucket&#x27;. Do not say that &#x27;trebuchet&#x27; always meant a counterweight machine; medieval usage was loose (see the mangonel card).","stop":3,"img":"villard-trebuchet.jpg","imgcap":"Villard de Honnecourt’s plan of a trebuchet base, about 1230. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-26","title":"Kublai Khan sends for Muslim engineers","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"1268–1273 (new engines used 1273)","place":"Xiangyang and Fancheng, on the Han River, China","text":"Xiangyang, on the Han River, was a key Song fortress blocking access to the Yangtze, and the Mongols besieged it for five years, from 1268 to 1273.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannica2026xiangyang|\" data-f=\"P3-F203 P3-F207\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In 1271 Kublai Khan sent to the Ilkhanate in Persia for engine-makers, and its ruler answered with two Muslim engineers, Alaowading and Yisimayin in Chinese (ʿAlāʾ al-Dīn and Ismāʿīl).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wuyungaowa2012mongol|;hanson2004mongol|\" data-f=\"P3-F204 P3-F205 P3-F206\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> According to the official Yuan history, Ismāʿīl surveyed the ground and set his engine at the south-east corner of Xiangyang; its shot weighed 150 jin, its discharge &#x27;shook heaven and earth&#x27;, it destroyed whatever it struck and it sank seven chi into the ground.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wuyungaowa2012mongol|\" data-f=\"P3-F204 P3-F205 P3-F206\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Fancheng fell early in 1273, and Xiangyang&#x27;s commander surrendered the same year.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"wuyungaowa2012mongol|;britannica2026xiangyang|\" data-f=\"P3-F203 P3-F204 P3-F205 P3-F206 P3-F207\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Marco Polo later claimed the decisive &#x27;great mangonels&#x27; were built to the Polos&#x27; specifications, a story Britannica calls dubious.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannica2026xiangyang|\" data-f=\"P3-F203 P3-F207\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"The counterweight trebuchet travelled east as well as west: China, where the traction trebuchet began, imported the heavier machine and its builders from the Islamic world. Marco Polo&#x27;s attempt to claim the credit is a lesson in reading sources.","quote":{"text":"置炮于城东南隅，重一百五十斤，机发，声震天地，所击无不摧陷，入地七尺","key":"wuyungaowa2012mongol","pin":"quoting Yuan shi 203 (biography of Yisimayin); our translation: 'he set the pao at the south-east corner of the city; [its shot] weighed 150 jin; when the mechanism was released the sound shook heaven and earth; whatever it struck was destroyed, and it sank seven chi into the ground'"},"sources":[{"key":"wuyungaowa2012mongol","grade":"A"},{"key":"britannica2026xiangyang","grade":"B"},{"key":"hanson2004mongol","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F203","P3-F204","P3-F205","P3-F206","P3-F207"],"caveats":"The Yuan shi was compiled in 1369-70, a century later; it is an official history and its figures are reported, not measured. The English rendering of the Chinese quote is ours, not a published translation; a scholarly English translation (e.g. in Rossabi 1988 or Franke) should be checked. &#x27;150 jin&#x27; is usually read as the projectile weight, but the sentence is terse. Hanson&#x27;s 75 kg conversion assumes a jin of about 0.5 kg; the Yuan jin may have been heavier, so treat the kg figure as approximate. Hanson&#x27;s De Re Militari essay (2004) is tier C (credentials unknown; relies on Rossabi). Hanson names the engineers as Isma&#x27;il of Hilla and Ala al-Din of Mosul; those home towns rest on Hanson alone, so they are left out of the retelling. Wuyungaowa writes the Ilkhan&#x27;s name as 阿不哥 and Hanson as Abakha (Abaqa); the identification is standard. Dates by Chinese lunar months: Wuyungaowa gives Fancheng in the first month and Xiangyang in the second month of 1273. Britannica&#x27;s text on Marco Polo appears on its Xiangyang page (possibly drawn from its Marco Polo article). The common label &#x27;Huihui pao&#x27; (&#x27;Muslim pao&#x27;) is used by Wuyungaowa (回回炮). We could not open the Yuan shi directly (ctext.org forbids automated access; Wikisource could not be fetched). Lead&#x27;s revision: Hanson&#x27;s 75 kg conversion was taken out of the retelling (single tier C source).","stop":3,"img":"wujing-traction.jpg","imgcap":"Five traction trebuchets on one base, Wujing Zongyao, 1044. Public domain."},{"id":"ST-27","title":"A Colossus paid for by siege engines","top10":false,"category":"Athens/ancient artillery","date":"305 BC (siege); Colossus c. 294–282 BC","place":"Rhodes","text":"In 305 BC Demetrius, son of Antigonus, attacked Rhodes; the failed siege won him the nickname Poliorcetes, ‘the Besieger’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannicanddemetrius|\" data-f=\"P3-F208\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> His greatest ‘city-taker’ (helepolis) was a tapering tower on a square base 48 cubits a side and 66 cubits high, with openings for missiles on every storey;<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"plutarch1920demetrius|21\" data-f=\"P3-F209\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> that is roughly 22 m by 30 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"marino2018rise|\" data-f=\"P3-F211 P3-F215\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Diodorus says 3,400 of the army’s strongest men were chosen to move it.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"diodorus1954library|20.91\" data-f=\"P3-F210 P3-F212\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> The siege failed and Demetrius made peace.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"diodorus1954library|20.99\" data-f=\"P3-F210 P3-F212\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Pliny says the Rhodians then spent twelve years and 300 talents building the 70-cubit bronze Colossus of the sun god, ‘a sum raised from the engines of warfare which had been abandoned by King Demetrius’.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"pliny1855natural|34.41;britannica2026colossus|\" data-f=\"P3-F213 P3-F214\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Modern writers add that some of the abandoned metal was probably recycled into the statue itself.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"higgins2024alternative|;marino2018rise|\" data-f=\"P3-F211 P3-F215\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"One of the Seven Wonders was, in effect, built out of a defeated siege train. It shows how valuable the iron, bronze and timber in ancient siege engines were.","quote":{"text":"a sum raised from the engines of warfare which had been abandoned by King Demetrius, when tired of the long-protracted siege of Rhodes","key":"pliny1855natural","pin":"34.41"},"sources":[{"key":"pliny1855natural","grade":"A"},{"key":"plutarch1920demetrius","grade":"A"},{"key":"diodorus1954library","grade":"A"},{"key":"britannicanddemetrius","grade":"B"},{"key":"britannica2026colossus","grade":"B"},{"key":"higgins2024alternative","grade":"B"},{"key":"marino2018rise","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F208","P3-F209","P3-F210","P3-F211","P3-F212","P3-F213","P3-F214","P3-F215"],"caveats":"Pliny (AD 77) writes &#x27;it is said&#x27; (Latin tradunt) some 370 years after the event. The lead&#x27;s wording &#x27;sold the engines for 300 talents&#x27; is not what Pliny says: he says the statue cost 300 talents, raised from Demetrius&#x27;s abandoned equipment (Latin &#x27;CCC talentis, quae contigerant ex apparatu regis Demetrii relicto&#x27;). Whether the equipment was sold, melted down, or both is unclear; Higgins says some was recycled and the rest sold, NatGeo says some bronze was scavenged from the helepolis. Helepolis dimensions differ between sources (Plutarch: 48 by 66 cubits; Diodorus: &#x27;almost fifty cubits&#x27; a side, nine storeys); metric conversions assume a cubit of about 0.46 m and are approximate (NatGeo: the helepolis &#x27;may have stood more than 100 feet tall&#x27;; 70 cubits about 108–110 feet). The figure of 3,400 men is Diodorus&#x27;s and may be inflated. End of siege conventionally 304 BC (not checked in an A/B source).","stop":2,"img":"sulla.jpg","imgcap":"A Roman portrait bust (an illustration). Public domain."},{"id":"ST-28","title":"Orban's gun and the end of an era","top10":false,"category":"Medieval siege","date":"April – 29 May 1453","place":"Constantinople (Istanbul)","text":"On 29 May 1453 the Ottomans under Mehmed II breached the ancient land wall of Constantinople after a 55-day siege, ending the Byzantine Empire.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"britannica2026constantinople|\" data-f=\"P3-F216\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Mehmed&#x27;s most famous weapon was a giant bombard made by Orban, a master craftsman from Hungary.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"kakaliagos2019damage|\" data-f=\"P3-F217 P3-F218 P3-F219 P3-F220 P3-F221\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In 2019 Aristotle Kakaliagos and Nikolaos Ninis reconstructed the gun from historical reports and modelled its bombardment of the Theodosian Walls using structural mechanics.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"kakaliagos2019damage|481\" data-f=\"P3-F217 P3-F218 P3-F219 P3-F220 P3-F221\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> They estimated a gunpowder charge of 177 kg, placed the gun 500 m from the inner walls, and found that their model reproduced the length of breach described in historical reports.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"kakaliagos2019damage|481\" data-f=\"P3-F217 P3-F218 P3-F219 P3-F220 P3-F221\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Their reconstruction fired a granite ball 752 mm across, weighing about 600 kg.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"kakaliagos2019damage|\" data-f=\"P3-F217 P3-F218 P3-F219 P3-F220 P3-F221\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> They also repeat reports that the gun eventually burst, killing Orban and its crew.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"kakaliagos2019damage|\" data-f=\"P3-F217 P3-F218 P3-F219 P3-F220 P3-F221\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"An engineering paper models the weapon that replaced the trebuchet with the same tools our paper uses, and its 600 kg ball outweighs anything a trebuchet in this story bank is reported to have thrown.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"britannica2026constantinople","grade":"B"},{"key":"kakaliagos2019damage","grade":"A"}],"facts":["P3-F216","P3-F217","P3-F218","P3-F219","P3-F220","P3-F221"],"caveats":"The gun&#x27;s dimensions and charge are modern estimates from historical reports (Barbaro, Kritoboulos, Chalkokondyles), not measurements; the paper&#x27;s text gives 177 kg in the abstract but 117 kg in a table, an inconsistency to check in the PDF. The 600 kg ball and 752 mm diameter come from the paper&#x27;s body, page not confirmed. The story that the gun burst and killed Orban is described by the authors as &#x27;historical reports&#x27; and is uncertain. We found no A/B source for trebuchets or other mechanical engines being used alongside the guns in 1453, so do not claim it. Chevedden et al. (1995, 71) note that the replacement of trebuchets by guns &#x27;took nearly two centuries and was not fully accomplished until metallic shot replaced stones&#x27;, which suits the Epilogue&#x27;s framing. The Britannica summary (by an editorial intern, fact-checked by Britannica editors) was all that loaded of that page.","stop":10,"img":"dardanelles-gun.jpg","imgcap":"The Dardanelles Gun of 1464, Fort Nelson. Photo Tim Sheerman-Chase, CC BY 2.0."},{"id":"ST-29","title":"The trebuchet that burned the boathouse","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"2005–2023","place":"Warwick Castle, Warwickshire, England","text":"In 2005 Warwick Castle installed a trebuchet designed by the Danish expert Peter Vemming and built of oak by a team of carpenters in Wiltshire.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"coventrytelegraph2005catapulted|;medievalhistories2015warwick|\" data-f=\"P3-F222 P3-F223 P3-F224 P3-F225 P3-F230\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It stood 18 m (60 ft) tall, weighed 22 tonnes and was billed as the world&#x27;s largest working siege machine.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"leamingtonobserver2015investigation|;ihbc2015warwick|\" data-f=\"P3-F222 P3-F225 P3-F226 P3-F227 P3-F228 P3-F229 P3-F230\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> On 10 April 2015, after some 6,500 firings, sparks from a flaming projectile set light to the thatched roof of the castle&#x27;s Victorian boathouse, built in 1896, in front of a crowd of visitors.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"leamingtonobserver2015investigation|;ihbc2015warwick|\" data-f=\"P3-F222 P3-F225 P3-F226 P3-F227 P3-F228 P3-F229 P3-F230\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Wear and tear later made the machine unsafe, and in 2023 it was replaced by a £500,000 copy built to the same specifications by the original builders, Carpenter Oak.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"smith2022warwick|;mingins2023warwick|\" data-f=\"P3-F225 P3-F231 P3-F232\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"A heritage-show trebuchet still managed to set fire to a building, a small modern echo of the incendiary use of siege engines, and its wear-out shows how hard repeated throwing is on timber.","quote":{"text":"years of launching heavy projectiles hundreds of metres took its toll and the wear and tear got to the point where it was no longer safe to operate","key":"smith2022warwick","pin":"quote of Liam Bartlett, operations director"},"sources":[{"key":"coventrytelegraph2005catapulted","grade":"C"},{"key":"medievalhistories2015warwick","grade":"C"},{"key":"leamingtonobserver2015investigation","grade":"C"},{"key":"ihbc2015warwick","grade":"C"},{"key":"smith2022warwick","grade":"C"},{"key":"mingins2023warwick","grade":"C"},{"key":"warwickcastlendmighty","grade":"C"}],"facts":["P3-F222","P3-F223","P3-F224","P3-F225","P3-F226","P3-F227","P3-F228","P3-F229","P3-F230","P3-F231","P3-F232"],"caveats":"All performance numbers are promotional claims printed by the press, not measurements: 150 kg to 300 m (2005), &#x27;up to 250m&#x27; (2022), &#x27;90kg projectiles 300 metres&#x27; (2023, for the new machine). Height is 18 m in most sources but 19 m in Medieval Histories; the castle&#x27;s own site now says &#x27;almost 60 feet&#x27; and &#x27;Britain&#x27;s largest working siege machine&#x27;, while earlier coverage said &#x27;world&#x27;s largest&#x27;. Middelaldercentret also claims &#x27;Europe&#x27;s, probably the world&#x27;s, largest trebuchet&#x27; – superlatives are unverifiable. The designer is named &#x27;Dr Peter Vemming&#x27; / &#x27;Peter Vemming from the medieval Centre in Nykøbing&#x27;; identifying him with Peter Vemming Hansen is our inference (very likely). The boathouse date is 1896 (Leamington Observer, BBC via IHBC, Medieval Histories) but 1895 in one Coventry Telegraph piece. The castle said the cause was not yet ascertained at the time; &#x27;sparks from the projectile&#x27; is the BBC/eyewitness account. The Coventry Telegraph 2005 article is undated online; the year is inferred from its content and later reports that the machine was built in 2005. The BBC original was blocked to the agent and read via the IHBC reproduction. Lead&#x27;s revision: the performance claims (150 kg to 300 m in 2005; &#x27;up to 250 m&#x27; in 2022) and &#x27;about 300 spectators&#x27; each rest on one C source and were taken out of the retelling.","stop":5,"img":"warwick-2.jpg","imgcap":"The Warwick Castle trebuchet. Photo Pahcal123, CC BY-SA 4.0."},{"id":"ST-30","title":"A dentist's pumpkin trebuchet passes half a mile","top10":false,"category":"Modern reconstruction/record","date":"2004–2016","place":"Greenfield, New Hampshire, and the World Championship Punkin Chunkin, Delaware, USA","text":"Steve Seigars, a dentist from Greenfield, New Hampshire, and the construction engineer Chuck Willard built a trebuchet called &#x27;Yankee Siege&#x27; to draw customers to Seigars&#x27;s farm stand.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nhbr2004pumpkin|;pratt2008pumpkin|;ap2016pumpkins|\" data-f=\"P3-F233 P3-F234 P3-F236 P3-F237 P3-F238 P3-F239\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It stood about 15–17 m (50–55 ft) tall on 3 m (10 ft) iron wheels.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nhbr2004pumpkin|;pratt2008pumpkin|\" data-f=\"P3-F233 P3-F234 P3-F236 P3-F237 P3-F238\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> It was designed to throw objects of up to 136 kg (300 lb), yet it made its name hurling pumpkins.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nhbr2004pumpkin|;npr2005pumpkin|\" data-f=\"P3-F233 P3-F234 P3-F237 P3-F238\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> At the 2004 World Championship Punkin Chunkin in Delaware it threw one 425 m (1,394 ft).<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"nhbr2004pumpkin|;npr2005pumpkin|\" data-f=\"P3-F233 P3-F234 P3-F237 P3-F238\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Its successor, Yankee Siege II, later held the trebuchet record of 864 m (2,835.81 ft), according to the American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"hyland2014pumpkin|\" data-f=\"P3-F239\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> In 2016 the Associated Press called Seigars an eight-time world champion who sent pumpkins &#x27;more than a half mile&#x27;.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"ap2016pumpkins|\" data-f=\"P3-F236 P3-F239\" data-label=\"Source\"></i> Air cannons go much farther: Guinness lists a pumpkin fired 1,690 m.<i class=\"mark\" data-s=\"guinnessndfarthest|\" data-f=\"P3-F235\" data-label=\"Source\"></i>","why":"Hobbyists optimising for one goal, distance, threw light projectiles far beyond the ranges reported for medieval machines, which shows how design priorities shape performance.","quote":null,"sources":[{"key":"nhbr2004pumpkin","grade":"C"},{"key":"pratt2008pumpkin","grade":"C"},{"key":"npr2005pumpkin","grade":"C"},{"key":"ap2016pumpkins","grade":"C"},{"key":"hyland2014pumpkin","grade":"C"},{"key":"guinnessndfarthest","grade":"B"}],"facts":["P3-F233","P3-F234","P3-F235","P3-F236","P3-F237","P3-F238","P3-F239"],"caveats":"&#x27;In about 2000&#x27; is inferred from &#x27;Eight years ago&#x27; in an August 2008 article; it fits NOVA&#x27;s February 2000 broadcast but the episode is not named. Height is 50 ft (NHBR 2004) or 55 ft (Yankee Magazine 2008). The 2004 throw was a record in its class according to Seigars. The 2,835.81 ft trebuchet record is from a single C source (AIAA communications, 2014); Atlas Obscura (tier D) gives &#x27;2,835 feet&#x27; but credits the original Yankee Siege. The year it was set is not stated. In 2016 Yankee Siege II won the trebuchet class at a New Hampshire contest with 2,608.44 ft (NHMS press release). Competition pumpkins (3.6–4.5 kg) are far lighter than medieval stones (tens to hundreds of kg), so these ranges are not comparable with historical machines. The Guinness figure (1,690.24 m, 2010) is for an air cannon, not a trebuchet; no Guinness trebuchet record was found. Lead&#x27;s revision: single-source details were moved out of the retelling: the NOVA inspiration and the 2005 record of 1,702 ft (Yankee Magazine only), the 40,000 lb mass and 8–10 lb contest pumpkins (NHBR only), and NPR&#x27;s 37 ft arm and 11,000 lb of rocks in the counterweight (NPR only). NPR calls the 2004 throw a world record.","stop":7,"img":"punkin-chunkin.jpg","imgcap":"A wheeled trebuchet at the 2008 World Championship Punkin Chunkin. Photo GB fan, CC BY-SA 3.0."}];
